/* Systems Engineering Course — data. window.CHAPTERS = 16 modules x 6 levels each. */
window.CHAPTERS = [

/* ============================ 01 · LINUX INTERNALS ============================ */
{ num: 1, emoji: '⚙️', title: 'Linux Internals & the Boot Path', layer: 'Foundation',
  tagline: 'What happens between power-on and a login prompt: firmware, the kernel, PID 1, the page cache, and how a CPU is shared.',
  apps: ['🖥️ every server you SSH into', '📉 "why is load 40?"', '🧠 "free shows no memory"'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>Press power and a relay race begins: <b>firmware</b> (UEFI/BIOS) tests the hardware and hands off to a <b>bootloader</b> (GRUB), which loads the <b>Linux kernel</b> and a small <b>initramfs</b> into RAM. The kernel mounts the real root filesystem and starts <b>PID 1</b> (<code>systemd</code>), which brings up every other service. Your shell is just one more process in that tree.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Opening a shop for the day: security unlocks the building (firmware), the manager reads the opening checklist (bootloader + kernel), turns on the lights and tills (initramfs → root fs), then staff take their stations (systemd units). Customers (your commands) only arrive once everyone is in place.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>A server that "hangs on boot" is almost always stuck at one baton pass — no bootable disk found (firmware), GRUB rescue prompt (bootloader), or <code>Give root password for maintenance</code> (a filesystem in <code>/etc/fstab</code> that would not mount).</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: read /proc/meminfo like the kernel does', '../linux/step1_proc_meminfo.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The kernel gives every process the same illusion: a private, contiguous <b>virtual address space</b> and a private view of the machine. Two mechanisms build it. <b>Virtual memory</b> (page tables + the MMU) maps 4 KiB pages of virtual addresses to physical RAM, swap, or a file — a <b>page fault</b> fills them in on demand. <b>The VFS</b> makes disks, sockets, devices and kernel state all look like files under one tree, so <code>cat /proc/cpuinfo</code> and <code>cat file.txt</code> use the identical syscalls.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Virtual memory is a hotel: every guest thinks they have room 100, the front desk (page tables) quietly maps each "room 100" to a different real room, and rooms are only cleaned and assigned when a guest actually walks in (demand paging).</p></div>' +
    '<p>A <b>minor</b> page fault wires up a page already in RAM (shared library, page cache hit); a <b>major</b> fault has to read from disk and is ~10,000× slower. High major-fault rate = you are thrashing.</p>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: page-cache & fault simulation', '../linux/step2_page_cache_sim.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>One CPU, hundreds of runnable threads — the <b>scheduler</b> decides who runs next. Linux used <b>CFS</b> (Completely Fair Scheduler) for a decade and moved to <b>EEVDF</b> in 6.6; both aim for the same thing: hand each task a fair slice of CPU-time, weighted by <code>nice</code>, and always run whoever is furthest behind. A context switch swaps registers, stack pointer and page-table base — cheap (~1–3 µs) but not free, which is why 100k switches/sec shows up as <code>%sy</code> in <code>top</code>.</p>' +
    '<pre><code># load average = avg number of tasks running OR waiting on D-state I/O\nuptime           # 0.7 0.9 1.1  → fine on 8 cores\n# load 40, CPU 3% idle-ish?  →  tasks stuck in uninterruptible I/O wait\nps -eo state,cmd | grep \'^D\'   # the culprits</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A single checkout lane with a fair cashier: everyone gets served in short bursts so nobody starves. "Load" counts people in the queue — if it is huge but the cashier is idle, the queue is full of people waiting on a price check from the back room (disk I/O), not on the cashier.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: a tiny fair scheduler', '../linux/step3_scheduler_sim.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Namespaces</b> and <b>cgroups</b> are how one kernel pretends to be many machines — the raw material of every container. A <b>namespace</b> gives a process a private view of one resource: <code>pid</code> (its own PID 1), <code>net</code> (its own interfaces and routing table), <code>mnt</code>, <code>uts</code> (hostname), <code>ipc</code>, <code>user</code> (UID 0 inside, unprivileged outside). A <b>cgroup</b> (v2: one unified hierarchy) accounts and caps CPU, memory, I/O and PIDs for a group of processes — exceed <code>memory.max</code> and the cgroup OOM-killer fires, not the global one.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p><code>systemd</code> already puts every service in its own cgroup — that is what <code>systemctl status sshd</code> means by "Tasks: 1, Memory: 6.4M". A runaway service hitting its <code>MemoryMax=</code> is killed in isolation instead of taking the box down.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 man 7 cgroups', 'https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/cgroups.7.html', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: performance work lives in the gap between the abstraction and the hardware. <b>The page cache</b> means "free" RAM is a myth — Linux uses spare memory to cache files and evicts it instantly under pressure, so <code>available</code> (not <code>free</code>) in <code>/proc/meminfo</code> is the number that matters. <b>Dirty page writeback</b> (<code>vm.dirty_ratio</code>) controls how much unwritten data can pile up before a write blocks. <b>NUMA</b> makes "RAM" have a distance — a page on the wrong socket costs ~1.5× latency (Module 12). And every syscall is a mode switch; <b>io_uring</b> and <b>eBPF</b> (Module 5) exist to do more work per crossing.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"The database got slow at 2 a.m." is often <code>vm.dirty_ratio</code> + a backup job: gigabytes of dirty pages hit the ratio, every write goes synchronous, latency spikes until writeback drains. The fix is tuning writeback thresholds, not more RAM.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Kernel docs: /proc/meminfo fields', 'https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/proc.html', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Three real 03:00 pages, and why each is (or is not) an incident.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — "2 GB free, it is about to OOM!"</span><p>A node running a big MPI job shows <code>MemFree</code> at 2 GB. It is fine: <code>free -m</code> also shows <code>available</code> = 96 GB — that is page cache Linux evicts the instant a process needs it, and <code>SwapUsed</code> is 0. <b>Fix the alert</b>: page on <code>MemAvailable/MemTotal</code>, plus <code>si/so</code> from <code>vmstat 1</code> and <code>pgmajfault</code> — non-zero and climbing is the real memory-pressure signal.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — load average 250, CPU 95% idle, every command hangs</span><p><code>ps -eo state,pid,cmd | awk \'$1~/D/\'</code> shows dozens of processes in uninterruptible sleep; <code>cat /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/stack</code> and <code>dmesg</code> point at <code>nfs: server /home not responding</code>. Load counts <b>D-state</b> tasks, and every process that <code>stat()</code>s a home dir — including your shell prompt — parks there. <code>kill -9</code> does nothing; they unblock only when the I/O completes or errors. Recover the storage; mount non-critical NFS <code>soft,timeo=,retrans=</code> so it errors instead of hanging forever.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Scenario — a DB stalls 200 ms every few minutes.</b> <code>vm.dirty_ratio=20</code> on a 512 GB box lets ~100 GB of dirty pages accumulate before writes go fully synchronous; when a backup or big <code>COPY</code> hits that ceiling, <i>every</i> writer (including the WAL) blocks in <code>balance_dirty_pages()</code>. Lower it: <code>dirty_background_ratio=3</code>, <code>dirty_ratio=10</code>, <code>dirty_expire_centisecs=1000</code> — then re-measure; do not cargo-cult the numbers.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: 5 production scenarios + node sysctl baseline', '../linux/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'What is the difference between a minor and a major page fault?', opts: ['Minor faults are warnings, major faults crash', 'Minor wires up a page already in RAM; major must read from disk/swap (~10,000× slower)', 'Major faults happen in the kernel, minor in userspace', 'They are the same thing at different priorities'], ok: 1, why: 'Both are the MMU asking the kernel to fill a mapping. Minor = page already resident (shared lib, cache) — cheap. Major = fetch from disk — the slow path that signals thrashing.' },
    { q: 'Load average is 40 on an 8-core box but CPU shows ~90% idle. Most likely cause?', opts: ['CPU is broken', 'Many tasks stuck in uninterruptible (D-state) I/O wait — they count toward load but do not use CPU', 'The scheduler is misconfigured', 'You need more RAM'], ok: 1, why: 'Linux load counts running + uninterruptible-sleep tasks. A storage or NFS stall parks dozens of processes in D-state: load soars while the CPU sits idle. Check `ps -eo state` and iostat.' },
    { q: 'In cgroups v2, what happens when a process group exceeds memory.max?', opts: ['The whole machine reboots', 'The global OOM-killer picks any process', 'The cgroup-scoped OOM-killer kills a task inside that cgroup only', 'The process is paused until memory frees'], ok: 2, why: 'cgroup v2 memory limits are enforced locally: breaching memory.max triggers OOM within that cgroup, isolating the blast radius from the rest of the system.' } ] },

/* ============================ 02 · SECURITY HARDENING ============================ */
{ num: 2, emoji: '🛡️', title: 'Security Hardening', layer: 'Security',
  tagline: 'Turning a default install into a defensible one: baselines, SSH/PAM, least-privilege sudo, MAC, sysctl, and how misconfig becomes root.',
  apps: ['🔐 every internet-facing host', '🧾 CIS / vendor audits', '🚨 "how did they get root?"'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>A fresh OS optimises for "works out of the box", not "survives the internet". <b>Hardening</b> is the systematic removal of that convenience: disable services you do not run, close ports you do not need, enforce strong auth, log what matters, and make privilege escalation loud and hard. You measure it against a <b>baseline</b> — the CIS Benchmark, a vendor guide, or a DISA STIG (Module 3).</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A new house comes with the builder\'s spare key under the mat, every window unlatched, and the alarm off "so you can move in easily". Hardening is your first weekend: change the locks, latch the windows, wire the alarm, and write down who has a key.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>The reason cloud images ship with <code>PermitRootLogin</code> already restricted and password auth off is that unhardened defaults were, for years, the single biggest source of compromised Linux hosts — bots find a new one within minutes.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: score a host against a CIS-style baseline', '../hardening/step1_cis_score.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>SSH is the front door, so it gets the most attention. The high-value settings in <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>: <code>PermitRootLogin no</code>, <code>PasswordAuthentication no</code> (keys only), <code>PubkeyAuthentication yes</code>, <code>AllowGroups ssh-users</code>, <code>MaxAuthTries 3</code>, modern <code>KexAlgorithms</code>/<code>Ciphers</code>/<code>MACs</code> only, and <code>ClientAliveInterval</code> for idle timeout. Behind it, <b>PAM</b> stacks the actual auth logic — <code>pam_faillock</code> (lockout), <code>pam_pwquality</code> (complexity), <code>pam_pwhistory</code>, and MFA modules.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><code>sshd_config</code> is the bouncer\'s dress code; PAM is the bouncer\'s full decision tree — check ID, check the banned list, count tonight\'s failed entries, demand a second factor. Change the dress code and forget the tree and people still walk in the side door.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: audit an sshd_config', '../hardening/step2_sshd_audit.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Least privilege</b> is the whole game. Don\'t hand out <code>sudo su -</code>; grant the exact commands a role needs in <code>/etc/sudoers.d/</code> with <code>Cmnd_Alias</code>, <code>NOPASSWD</code> only where unavoidable, and <code>!</code> to blacklist shell escapes. Every wildcard is a hole: <code>sudo vi</code> spawns a root shell with <code>:!sh</code>; <code>sudo tar --checkpoint-action=exec</code> runs arbitrary code; <code>sudo less</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>awk</code>, <code>systemctl</code> (pager!) all escape. GTFOBins is the catalogue attackers use.</p>' +
    '<pre><code># bad: one wildcard = full root\n%ops ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl *\n# better: exact units, no pager escape\n%ops ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx, /usr/bin/systemctl status nginx\nDefaults!/usr/bin/systemctl env_keep -= "SYSTEMD_PAGER"</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Giving a contractor a master key "to save time" instead of the one door they are working on. The master key is fine until the day it isn\'t — and you will never know which contractor copied it.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: find privilege-escalation paths', '../hardening/step3_privesc_paths.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Discretionary permissions (owner/group/other) can be granted away by their owner. <b>Mandatory Access Control</b> can\'t: <b>SELinux</b> (RHEL) or <b>AppArmor</b> (Ubuntu/SUSE) confine each process to a policy the admin sets, so a compromised web server still cannot read <code>/etc/shadow</code> even though it "could" by file mode. SELinux is label-based and stricter (<code>type enforcement</code>, <code>booleans</code>, <code>audit2allow</code> to build policy from denials); AppArmor is path-based and easier to reason about. Kernel <b>sysctl</b> hardening backs it up: <code>kernel.kptr_restrict</code>, <code>kernel.yama.ptrace_scope</code>, <code>net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter</code>, <code>kernel.randomize_va_space=2</code>.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"Just run <code>setenforce 0</code> to fix it" is the most expensive shortcut in enterprise Linux — it disables the one control that would have contained the next exploit. The right move is <code>ausearch -m avc | audit2allow</code> and a targeted policy module.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 SELinux for mere mortals', 'https://opensource.com/article/18/7/sysadmin-guide-selinux', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: hardening a host is necessary and insufficient. Add <b>defense in depth</b> — <code>auditd</code> rules mapped to a framework (watch <code>/etc/</code>, <code>execve</code>, privileged syscalls) shipped to a SIEM; <b>file integrity</b> (AIDE) with an off-box database; <b>immutable</b> attributes (<code>chattr +i</code>) on key configs; <code>nftables</code> default-deny inbound; and <b>secure boot + IMA/EVM</b> so the kernel and critical files are measured. Then <b>verify continuously</b>: a baseline you set once and never re-scan has already drifted. Threat-model the crown jewels and assume the perimeter is already breached.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Post-incident reviews almost never say "they beat our hardening". They say "the hardening was correct on day 1, nobody re-scanned, and 14 months of changes opened three holes". Compliance-as-code (Module 3) exists to close that loop.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 CIS Benchmarks', 'https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>How hardening actually goes wrong in production — three patterns.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — compromise via a "harmless" sudo rule</span><p><code>%deploy ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl *</code>. A leaked CI token runs <code>sudo systemctl status</code> → the pager (<code>less</code>) opens as root → <code>!/bin/sh</code> → root shell. <b>Fix</b>: exact unit names, no wildcard, <code>env_reset</code>, kill <code>SYSTEMD_PAGER</code>. <b>Prevent</b>: a CI grep of <code>sudoers.d/</code> for <code>*</code>, <code>ALL</code>, and <code>NOPASSWD.*(vi|less|man|find|awk|tar|env|python)</code> that fails the pipeline.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — "we disabled root login" and still got breached</span><p><code>PermitRootLogin no</code> was set, but <code>PasswordAuthentication yes</code> stayed on and a <code>jenkins</code> service account had a weak password + a shell. Fix: <code>PasswordAuthentication no</code>, <code>AuthenticationMethods publickey</code>, <code>AllowGroups interactive-users</code> (service accounts excluded, <code>nologin</code> shell, <code>passwd -l</code>). <code>fail2ban</code> is depth, not the control. And <code>prohibit-password</code> still allows a root <i>key</i> — use <code>no</code>.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Scenario — hardening broke the cluster.</b> Full CIS L2 remediation: <code>noexec</code> on <code>/tmp</code> and <code>/dev/shm</code> kills MPI, Python venvs and Spack builds; <code>unprivileged_userns_clone=0</code> breaks Apptainer; FIPS retrofitted on a running box won\'t reboot. <b>Reconciliation</b>: tailor per node role (login vs compute vs storage), document each deviation with a justification + compensating control, apply <code>--check</code> first, canary one node, then roll. "96% compliant with 11 documented exceptions" beats "100% and the cluster is down".</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: sudo escapes, SELinux fleet fix, auditd rules, the hardening role', '../hardening/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why grant specific commands in sudoers instead of full sudo?', opts: ['It is faster to type', 'Least privilege: many common binaries (vi, less, tar, find, systemctl pager) can spawn a root shell, so any wildcard grant is effectively full root', 'sudo is deprecated', 'It uses less memory'], ok: 1, why: 'GTFOBins-style shell escapes mean a broad or wildcarded sudo rule is equivalent to root. Enumerate exact commands, block pagers/editors, and avoid NOPASSWD where you can.' },
    { q: 'What does Mandatory Access Control (SELinux/AppArmor) add over Unix file permissions?', opts: ['Faster file access', 'A policy the file owner cannot override — a compromised process stays confined even if file modes would allow more', 'Encryption of files at rest', 'It replaces the need for a firewall'], ok: 1, why: 'DAC lets an owner (or an exploited process running as that owner) widen access. MAC enforces an admin-set policy on top, containing lateral movement after a compromise.' },
    { q: 'A host passed its CIS scan at deployment. Best practice for keeping it hardened?', opts: ['Nothing — it is done', 'Re-scan on a schedule and gate config changes through compliance-as-code; baselines drift as the system is used', 'Disable SELinux so scans run faster', 'Only re-scan after a breach'], ok: 1, why: 'Configuration drift is continuous. Automated periodic scanning plus policy-as-code (Ansible/OpenSCAP) is what keeps a system at its accredited baseline over its lifetime.' } ] },

/* ============================ 03 · STIGs & COMPLIANCE ============================ */
{ num: 3, emoji: '📋', title: 'STIGs & Compliance Automation', layer: 'Security',
  tagline: 'How the DoD ships a hardened OS: the XCCDF/OVAL data model, CAT I/II/III, OpenSCAP scanning and remediation, tailoring, and staying accredited.',
  apps: ['🏛️ government / regulated systems', '🧾 audit season', '🤖 compliance-as-code pipelines'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>A <b>STIG</b> (Security Technical Implementation Guide) is DISA\'s configuration standard for a product — "here is exactly how a RHEL 9 box must be set to be allowed on a DoD network". It is the same idea as a CIS Benchmark, but contractual: an accreditor signs off on it. Each STIG is a list of <b>rules</b>, each with a check ("how to tell if you comply") and a fix ("how to comply").</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A restaurant health code. CIS is the well-regarded industry handbook you choose to follow; a STIG is the inspector\'s checklist you must pass to keep the doors open, item by item, with a grade posted in the window.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>If you have ever seen a login banner that reads "You are accessing a U.S. Government information system..." — that exact wording is a STIG rule, and a scanner checks it byte for byte.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: parse an XCCDF rule set', '../stigs/step1_parse_xccdf.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The machine-readable stack is <b>SCAP</b> (Security Content Automation Protocol). <b>XCCDF</b> is the checklist language — rules, groups, profiles, severities. <b>OVAL</b> is the assertion language — "the actual test": <code>textfilecontent54</code> to grep a config, <code>rpmverify</code> for package state, <code>sysctl</code> objects, etc. A <b>profile</b> selects a subset of rules for a context (e.g. <code>stig</code>, <code>cui</code>, <code>ospp</code>). Severity is <b>CAT I</b> (would directly allow compromise — fix now), <b>CAT II</b> (contributes — fix soon), <b>CAT III</b> (hardening depth).</p>' +
    '<pre><code>oscap xccdf eval \\\n  --profile xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_profile_stig \\\n  --results scan.xml --report scan.html \\\n  /usr/share/xml/scap/ssg/content/ssg-rhel9-ds.xml</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>XCCDF is the exam paper (questions, marks per question, which questions this candidate sits); OVAL is the marking scheme that says precisely what a correct answer looks like. <code>oscap</code> is the invigilator who runs it and prints the grade sheet.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: roll up CAT I/II/III pass rates', '../stigs/step2_severity_rollup.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>You don\'t hand-write STIG content — you use <b>ComplianceAsCode / SCAP Security Guide (SSG)</b>, the open-source project that ships XCCDF+OVAL <i>and</i> matching remediation as <b>Bash</b> and <b>Ansible</b> for RHEL, Ubuntu, SLES, Fedora, and more. Workflow: scan → review → remediate → re-scan. Remediation via Ansible is preferred because it is idempotent and re-runnable (Module 6):</p>' +
    '<pre><code># generate a targeted playbook from a scan\'s failures\noscap xccdf generate fix --fix-type ansible \\\n  --result-id \'\' scan.xml &gt; remediate.yml\nansible-playbook -i inventory remediate.yml --check   # dry run first</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Blindly applying every fix will break things — auto-remediation has bricked sudo, locked out SSH, and killed FIPS boots. Real teams run the remediation in <code>--check</code>, diff it, stage it, and keep a documented exception list.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: simulate an OVAL evaluation', '../stigs/step3_oval_eval_sim.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Real systems can\'t pass 100% as-is, so you <b>tailor</b>. A <b>tailoring file</b> (built with <code>scap-workbench</code> or by hand) disables or modifies rules for your environment; each deviation needs a justification. What you cannot fix becomes a <b>POA&amp;M</b> (Plan of Action &amp; Milestones) — documented risk, compensating control, remediation date — which the <b>Authorizing Official</b> accepts as part of granting an <b>ATO</b> (Authority to Operate) under the <b>RMF</b> process. STIG findings map to <b>NIST 800-53</b> controls, which is how one scan feeds many frameworks (FedRAMP, CMMC, PCI).</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A building that can\'t meet a new fire code everywhere files for a variance: "this stairwell is grandfathered, we added sprinklers as a compensating control, full retrofit is scheduled for Q3". The POA&amp;M is that variance, and the AO is the fire marshal who signs it.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 DISA STIG library', 'https://public.cyber.mil/stigs/', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: compliance is a <b>pipeline property</b>, not an event. Bake SSG hardening into the <b>image</b> (Warewulf node image, cloud AMI, container base) so nodes are born compliant; run <code>oscap</code> in <b>CI</b> and fail the build on new CAT I; schedule <b>drift scans</b> and alert on regressions; store results as evidence with timestamps. Watch the sharp edges: <b>FIPS mode</b> must be enabled at install (<code>fips-mode-setup</code>) not retrofitted; <b>container</b> STIGs differ from host STIGs; <b>kernel</b> rules (audit backlog, <code>init_on_alloc</code>) need a reboot; and STIG + vendor + local policy will contradict each other — you need a documented precedence order.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>The teams that dread audits scan by hand the week before. The teams that don\'t have a dashboard that has said "RHEL9 STIG: 96.4%, 0 CAT I, 11 accepted POA&amp;Ms" every day for a year — the audit is just exporting that.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 ComplianceAsCode / SSG', 'https://github.com/ComplianceAsCode/content', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Compliance under real deadlines and real contradictions.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — audit in three weeks, last scan "sometime last year"</span><p>Playbook: (1) baseline every host — push <code>scap-security-guide</code> via Ansible, <code>oscap xccdf eval --profile stig</code>, collect <code>results.xml</code> centrally; (2) roll up per-host and fleet CAT I/II/III pass rates; (3) fix <b>all</b> open CAT I (target: zero), batch the repeating CAT II; (4) remediate in waves — <code>oscap xccdf generate fix</code>, <code>--check</code> diff, canary, roll, re-scan; (5) document the rest as POA&amp;Ms with dates; (6) freeze + snapshot dated scans as the evidence package.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — auto-remediation locked everyone out of SSH</span><p>Someone ran the generated <code>remediate.yml</code> without <code>--check</code>. It set <code>AllowGroups</code> to a non-existent group and reloaded <code>sshd</code>; the box had no console. <b>Recovery</b>: IPMI SOL / iDRAC, single-user, revert from the pre-change backup the remediation should have taken. <b>Permanent fix</b>: <code>sshd</code> changes only via a handler that runs <code>sshd -t</code> before reload; <code>--check --diff</code> in CI with human approval; a documented break-glass account.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Scenario — STIG vs vendor vs local policy contradict.</b> STIG wants <code>noexec /tmp</code>; Spack needs <code>exec</code>. STIG wants <code>unprivileged_userns_clone=0</code>; Apptainer needs <code>1</code>. Resolution: a written <b>precedence order</b> (STIG &gt; vendor hard-requirement &gt; local convenience), every deviation in a tailoring file / POA&amp;M with a technical justification, and different tailoring per node role. FIPS must be set <i>at install</i>, never retrofitted.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: audit sprint, FIPS, container STIGs, day-2 pipeline', '../stigs/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'What is the relationship between XCCDF and OVAL in SCAP?', opts: ['They are competing standards', 'XCCDF is the checklist (rules, profiles, severities); OVAL is the low-level test logic each rule calls to decide pass/fail', 'OVAL is for Windows, XCCDF for Linux', 'XCCDF replaced OVAL'], ok: 1, why: 'XCCDF structures the benchmark and selection; OVAL defines the concrete machine state assertions. oscap executes XCCDF, which references OVAL definitions.' },
    { q: 'A CAT I finding means:', opts: ['Cosmetic / documentation only', 'A weakness that directly and immediately allows compromise — highest priority to remediate', 'Category one of ten optional tiers', 'It only applies to classified systems'], ok: 1, why: 'DISA severity: CAT I = direct path to loss of confidentiality/integrity/availability, CAT II = contributes to it, CAT III = defense-in-depth. Accreditors expect zero open CAT I.' },
    { q: 'The system cannot meet a specific STIG rule without breaking a required app. Correct action?', opts: ['Ignore it silently', 'Turn off the scanner for that host', 'Document it in a POA&M with a compensating control and remediation date for the Authorizing Official to accept', 'Fail the whole accreditation'], ok: 2, why: 'Tailoring + POA&M is the sanctioned process: record the deviation, justify it, add compensating controls, set a milestone. The AO accepts residual risk as part of the ATO.' } ] },

/* ============================ 04 · HPC BENCHMARKING ============================ */
{ num: 4, emoji: '📊', title: 'HPC Benchmarking', layer: 'Measurement',
  tagline: 'Measuring a machine honestly: HPL vs HPCG, STREAM and the memory wall, OSU latency/bandwidth, IOR for storage, roofline, and Amdahl vs Gustafson.',
  apps: ['🏆 Top500 submissions', '🛒 acceptance testing a new cluster', '🐌 "why does it not scale?"'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>You cannot tune, size, or buy what you cannot measure. A <b>benchmark</b> is a workload with a known shape run under controlled conditions to produce one comparable number. The trap: every benchmark measures <i>one</i> thing, and vendors quote the flattering one. Know what each stresses.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Judging a car by 0–60 tells you nothing about towing capacity, fuel economy, or how it handles a mountain pass. "It does 2 petaflops" is a 0–60 time — impressive, and possibly irrelevant to your actual commute.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>The <b>Top500</b> list ranks supercomputers by <b>HPL</b> (dense linear algebra — very cache/FLOP friendly). The <b>HPCG</b> list ranks the same machines by a sparse solver closer to real science, and the order changes dramatically: many machines get 1–3% of their HPL number on HPCG.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: size HPL (problem size N) for a cluster', '../benchmarking/step1_hpl_problem_size.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The core benchmarks and what they prove:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>HPL     dense LU solve   → peak FP64, the Top500 number\nHPCG    sparse CG        → memory + comms bound, "real science" floor\nSTREAM  a[i]=b[i]+s*c[i] → sustainable memory bandwidth (GB/s)\nOSU     MPI pingpong     → point-to-point latency (µs) & bandwidth\nIOR     parallel I/O     → filesystem read/write GB/s at scale\nmdtest  file create/stat → metadata ops/s\nfio     block I/O        → IOPS / latency of a device or mount</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A gym assessment: HPL is a one-rep max (raw power), STREAM is how fast you can carry groceries up stairs all day (sustained throughput), OSU latency is your reaction time, IOR is how fast the whole team can move boxes through one doorway.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>STREAM</b> matters most because most HPC codes are <b>memory-bandwidth bound</b>: a modern core can do far more FLOPs than the memory system can feed it operands for.</p>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: STREAM bandwidth & the roofline', '../benchmarking/step2_stream_roofline.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The <b>roofline model</b> puts it on one chart: x-axis = <b>arithmetic intensity</b> (FLOPs per byte moved from memory), y-axis = achievable GFLOP/s. Two ceilings: a slanted line = <code>bandwidth × intensity</code> (memory bound), a flat line = peak compute (compute bound). Your kernel sits at its intensity; whichever ceiling is lower is your limit. Low intensity (stencils, SpMV, BLAS-1) → buy bandwidth or improve reuse. High intensity (dense GEMM, BLAS-3) → you can actually use those FLOPs.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"We added GPUs and it barely got faster" almost always means the code was memory-bound at intensity ~0.5 FLOP/byte. The GPU raised the flat ceiling nobody was hitting; the slanted ceiling (bandwidth) is what needed to move.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Roofline: an insightful visual model', 'https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1498765.1498785', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Scaling</b> has two questions. <b>Strong scaling</b>: fixed problem, more ranks — bounded by <b>Amdahl</b>: <code>speedup ≤ 1 / (s + (1−s)/N)</code>, so 5% serial caps you at 20× no matter the node count. <b>Weak scaling</b>: grow the problem with the ranks — <b>Gustafson</b> says this stays near-linear if the serial part does not grow. Report <b>efficiency</b> (<code>speedup / N</code>), not just speedup, and always plot it. The knee in the curve is usually communication: halo exchange, <code>MPI_Allreduce</code>, or load imbalance.</p>' +
    '<pre><code># strong scaling, 5% serial:\nN=8   → 5.9×   (74% eff)\nN=64  → 15.4×  (24% eff)\nN=512 → 19.3×  (3.8% eff)  ← stop here; you are burning nodes</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Nine women cannot make a baby in one month (Amdahl — some work is serial). But nine women can have nine babies in nine months (Gustafson — scale the problem, not the schedule).</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: Amdahl / Gustafson scaling curves', '../benchmarking/step3_amdahl_scaling.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: a benchmark result without a <b>methodology</b> is a rumour. Pin frequency (disable turbo drift) and record it; bind processes (Module 12) and report the map; warm caches or explicitly measure cold; run ≥5 times and report median + spread, not the best; hold the node exclusively; capture BIOS/firmware, microcode, kernel, compiler flags, MPI, and math library versions. For acceptance testing a new cluster, define pass criteria <i>before</i> delivery (e.g. "≥ 85% of theoretical STREAM on every node, HPL within 3% of quote, OSU latency ≤ 1.1 µs intra-rack") and run the whole fleet — one slow node (bad DIMM, throttling, wrong NUMA) hides in an average and shows up as a straggler in every job for years.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>The classic new-cluster bug: 3% of nodes run 15% slow. Cause list, in order of frequency: thermal throttling (dust/fan), a DIMM that fell to a lower speed, <code>cpupower</code> governor left on <code>powersave</code>, or a firmware rev mismatch. A per-node STREAM sweep finds all four.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 HPL & HPCG (netlib / official)', 'https://www.netlib.org/benchmark/hpl/', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>What a real acceptance test and a real scaling investigation look like.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — 240-node delivery, 4% of nodes slow</span><p>Whole-cluster HPL passes at 2% under quote, but a <b>per-node</b> STREAM sweep finds 9 nodes at 78–82% while the rest hit 94–96%. In order of frequency: thermal throttling (dusty heatsink — <code>turbostat</code> shows clock sag), one DIMM at a lower speed (<code>dmidecode -t 17</code> — STREAM drops ~1/6), governor left on <code>powersave</code>, microcode skew, wrong Sub-NUMA-Clustering BIOS setting. <b>One slow node is a straggler in every MPI job it ever lands in</b> — add a per-node STREAM + single-node HPL gate to image acceptance and run it after every physical touch.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — "the code does not scale past 128 nodes"</span><p>Speedup plateaus at ~40× on 128 nodes, then <i>drops</i> at 256. <code>mpiP</code>/Score-P shows the time going into <code>MPI_Allreduce</code> in the inner loop (cost grows with rank count) and load imbalance (the reduction waits for the slowest rank). Fixes: batch reductions, <code>MPI_Iallreduce</code> + overlap, non-blocking halo exchange, parallel start-up I/O. Plot <b>efficiency</b>, fit Amdahl\'s <code>s</code>: if <code>s≈0.02</code> the cap is 50× and more nodes is the wrong move — switch to weak scaling.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>The harness that makes a number defensible</b>: <code>--exclusive</code>, fixed frequency (recorded), <code>drop_caches</code>, THP=never, <code>numa_balancing=0</code>, first-touch pinning, ≥ 11 runs, report <b>median ± spread</b> with a provenance file (BIOS, microcode, cmdline, modules) and the exact command. Anything less is not reproducible.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: acceptance, HPL/STREAM tuning, variance, the honest harness', '../benchmarking/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why can two supercomputers swap ranking between the Top500 (HPL) and HPCG lists?', opts: ['The lists use different hardware', 'HPL is dense/compute-friendly and hits near peak FLOPs; HPCG is sparse and memory/communication bound, so it reflects a different bottleneck', 'HPCG is a synthetic fake', 'One list is older'], ok: 1, why: 'HPL flatters FLOP-rich machines; HPCG stresses memory bandwidth and network collectives, closer to real sparse solvers. Machines optimised for one need not lead the other.' },
    { q: 'A kernel has arithmetic intensity ~0.5 FLOP/byte. Adding faster compute units barely helps. Why?', opts: ['The compiler is broken', 'It is memory-bandwidth bound — it sits on the slanted roofline ceiling, so only more bandwidth or better data reuse raises performance', 'Arithmetic intensity does not affect performance', 'It needs more disk'], ok: 1, why: 'Low intensity means performance = bandwidth × intensity. Raising peak FLOPs lifts a ceiling you were never touching; you must move the bandwidth ceiling or increase reuse (blocking, fusion).' },
    { q: 'Your code is 5% serial. What is the hard upper bound on strong-scaling speedup?', opts: ['100×', 'About 20× (1 / 0.05), by Amdahl\'s law — regardless of how many nodes you add', 'Unlimited with enough nodes', '5×'], ok: 1, why: 'Amdahl: speedup ≤ 1/s as N→∞. s = 0.05 → max 20×. Beyond a point you add nodes and efficiency collapses; weak scaling (Gustafson) is the way out if the science allows a bigger problem.' } ] },

/* ============================ 05 · eBPF & OBSERVABILITY ============================ */
{ num: 5, emoji: '🐝', title: 'eBPF & Observability', layer: 'Observability',
  tagline: 'Safe programs inside the kernel: the verifier, maps and the ring buffer, tracepoints vs kprobes vs fentry, CO-RE/BTF, and building a latency histogram.',
  apps: ['🔍 bcc / bpftrace one-liners', '📈 Cilium, Pixie, Parca, Falco', '🕵️ "which process opened that file?"'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p><b>eBPF</b> lets you load a small program into the running kernel that fires on an event — a syscall, a function entry, a packet, a tracepoint — collects data, and hands it to userspace, all without patching the kernel or loading a module. It is how modern tools answer "what is the kernel actually doing right now?" with almost no overhead.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Instead of shutting the factory to install a permanent camera (a kernel module) or guessing from the output pile (logs), you clip a tiny, pre-approved sensor onto one machine while it runs. It can only do what the safety inspector allowed, and you unclip it when done.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p><code>bpftrace -e \'tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_openat { printf("%s %s\\n", comm, str(args-&gt;filename)); }\'</code> prints every file opened, by which command, system-wide, live. Ten years ago that needed <code>strace</code> on one PID at a time and a big performance hit.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: why the verifier rejects your program', '../ebpf/step1_verifier_sim.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The kernel will run your code only if it can <b>prove</b> it is safe. The <b>verifier</b> walks every possible path and rejects: unbounded loops (bounded loops OK since 5.3), out-of-bounds memory access, dereferencing a pointer without a NULL check, reading uninitialised stack, exceeding 1M instructions, or touching kernel memory you were not handed. This is why eBPF cannot crash or hang the kernel — the price is that the compiler (via <code>clang -target bpf</code>) and your code must stay inside what the verifier can reason about.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A customs officer who checks every branch of your suitcase before you board — not "looks fine", but opens every compartment and every possible packing order. Nothing questionable gets on the plane, but you can only pack what the rules clearly permit.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 BPF design Q&A / verifier', 'https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/bpf/verifier.html', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>eBPF programs are event handlers with no <code>printf</code> to a terminal — they talk to userspace through <b>maps</b>. A <b>hash map</b> keeps state between events (per-PID start timestamps). A <b>per-CPU array</b> avoids locks for counters. A <b>histogram</b> is a map of buckets. Streaming events use the <b>ring buffer</b> (BPF_MAP_TYPE_RINGBUF, 5.8+) — one shared MPSC buffer, ordered, efficient, replacing the older per-CPU perf buffer.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>// entry:  starts[pid] = ktime\n// return: delta = ktime - starts[pid];  hist[log2(delta)]++\n// userspace polls the map every second and prints the histogram</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Maps are the pigeonholes on the office wall: the kernel-side worker drops notes in labelled slots, the userspace reader empties them on a schedule. The ring buffer is the pneumatic tube for messages that must arrive in order, right now.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: a ring buffer, from scratch', '../ebpf/step2_ring_buffer.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Where you can attach, from most to least stable: <b>tracepoints</b> (static, kernel-maintained, stable ABI — prefer these), <b>fentry/fexit</b> (BPF trampolines on any function, faster than kprobes, needs BTF), <b>kprobes/kretprobes</b> (dynamic, attach anywhere, but the symbol may vanish next release), <b>uprobes</b> (userspace functions), <b>LSM hooks</b> (security decisions), <b>XDP/tc</b> (packet processing). Portability comes from <b>CO-RE</b> (Compile Once – Run Everywhere): the program references struct fields through <b>BTF</b> type info and libbpf relocates offsets at load time, so one binary runs across kernel versions without the bcc "compile LLVM on every host" tax.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Cilium replaces kube-proxy iptables with eBPF at the tc/XDP layer; Falco watches syscalls via eBPF for runtime security; Parca/Pixie do continuous profiling. All of them ship CO-RE binaries so they load on your kernel without a toolchain.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 BPF CO-RE reference', 'https://nakryiko.com/posts/bpf-core-reference-guide/', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: eBPF is now a <b>platform</b>, not a tracing trick. Know the limits — verifier complexity ceilings on big programs, per-hook overhead (a kretprobe on a hot function <i>does</i> cost), sampling vs full capture trade-offs, and the security surface (<code>CAP_BPF</code>, unprivileged eBPF mostly disabled). For HPC specifically: use it to catch stragglers (per-rank syscall latency), noisy-neighbour I/O, TCP retransmits on the management net, and off-CPU time (<code>offcputime</code>) — the "my job did nothing for 8 seconds" mystery is usually a lock, a page fault storm, or a filesystem stall that off-CPU profiling shows instantly.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p><code>biolatency</code>, <code>execsnoop</code>, <code>tcpretrans</code>, <code>runqlat</code>, <code>offcputime</code> — five bcc/bpftrace tools that resolve the majority of "the box feels slow" tickets in minutes without installing an agent or restarting anything.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: syscall latency histogram', '../ebpf/step3_latency_hist.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>eBPF earning its keep on problems nothing else can see live.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — one rank in a 512-rank job is 3× slow, randomly</span><p>Wall time swings 2–3× between identical runs; one rank is always late to <code>MPI_Allreduce</code>. On the suspect node <b>while the job runs</b>: <code>offcputime -p PID</code> (where is it blocked off-CPU — <code>io_schedule</code>? a <code>futex</code>? a page-fault storm?), <code>funclatency \'t:syscalls:sys_enter_futex\'</code>, <code>biolatency</code>, <code>tcpretrans</code>. Findings that turn up: seconds in <code>io_schedule</code> → a filesystem stall; huge <code>futex</code> time → an MPI progress thread fighting compute threads for a core; <code>tcpretrans</code> firing → a marginal fabric link. <b>No agent, no job restart.</b></p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — node load high, top shows nothing</span><p><code>execsnoop -T</code> reveals a node-health-check or a security scanner re-forking every second; or <code>runqlat</code> shows the node running 2× more threads than cores (a job spawned <code>OMP_NUM_THREADS=128</code> on a 64-core node). <code>profile -F 99 -adf 10</code> → a flamegraph of where the CPU actually goes.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>The honest costs</b>: the verifier\'s ~1M-instruction ceiling (split into tail-called sub-programs); a <code>kretprobe</code> on a very hot function (<code>vfs_read</code> on a busy fileserver) <i>does</i> cost measurable CPU — sample, or use the cheaper <code>fentry</code> trampoline; ring buffer needs kernel 5.8, CO-RE/<code>fentry</code> need BTF (<code>bpftool feature probe</code>); and unprivileged eBPF is disabled by a hardening sysctl you keep.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: straggler hunts, runtime security, kube-proxy replacement, limits', '../ebpf/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why can an eBPF program not crash or hang the kernel?', opts: ['It runs in a VM with a timeout', 'The in-kernel verifier statically proves termination and memory safety on every path before the program is allowed to load', 'The kernel ignores errors from it', 'It runs as a separate process'], ok: 1, why: 'The verifier rejects unbounded loops, OOB access, unchecked pointers and uninitialised reads at load time. If it cannot prove safety, the program does not run — that is the core guarantee.' },
    { q: 'How does an eBPF program pass collected data to userspace?', opts: ['It writes to /var/log', 'Through maps (hash, per-CPU array, histogram) and the ring buffer, which userspace reads/polls', 'It calls printf directly to the terminal', 'It cannot — eBPF is write-only'], ok: 1, why: 'eBPF has no stdio. Maps hold state and counters; the ring buffer streams ordered events. Userspace (libbpf/bcc/bpftrace) opens the map fd and reads it.' },
    { q: 'You need a stable attach point that will not break on the next kernel upgrade. Choose:', opts: ['A kretprobe on an internal function', 'A static tracepoint (kernel-maintained stable ABI), ideally with a CO-RE binary', 'Patch the kernel source', 'A uprobe on libc'], ok: 1, why: 'Tracepoints are curated and stable; kprobes attach to symbols that can be renamed or inlined away. CO-RE + BTF then makes the compiled program portable across kernel versions.' } ] },

/* ============================ 06 · ANSIBLE ============================ */
{ num: 6, emoji: '🔧', title: 'Ansible Automation at Scale', layer: 'Automation',
  tagline: 'Configuration as a repeatable fact: inventory and var precedence, real idempotency, handlers, rolling updates with health gates, and performance.',
  apps: ['🏗️ provisioning + config mgmt', '🔁 patch Tuesdays across 2,000 hosts', '🧯 "the playbook is not safe to re-run"'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p><b>Ansible</b> describes the <i>desired state</i> of a machine in YAML and makes it so over SSH — no agent to install. A <b>playbook</b> maps <b>plays</b> (a set of hosts) to <b>tasks</b> (calls to <b>modules</b> like <code>package</code>, <code>copy</code>, <code>systemd</code>, <code>lineinfile</code>). You run it, it reports <code>changed</code> or <code>ok</code> per task per host.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A recipe written as "the cake should end up frosted", not "spread frosting". If the cake is already frosted, that step does nothing. You can hand the same recipe to the kitchen every morning and only the un-done steps happen.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Warewulf, Slurm, Lustre clients, the STIG remediation from Module 3, monitoring agents, users and SSH keys across a whole cluster — all typically one <code>ansible-playbook site.yml</code> away, versioned in git, reviewed like code.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: resolve inventory & variable precedence', '../ansible/step1_inventory_graph.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Inventory</b> lists hosts and groups them (<code>[compute]</code>, <code>[login]</code>, <code>[gpu:children]</code>). Variables can be set in ~22 places; the ones you must know, low → high priority: role defaults → <code>group_vars/all</code> → <code>group_vars/&lt;group&gt;</code> → <code>host_vars/&lt;host&gt;</code> → play <code>vars</code> → <code>-e</code> extra-vars (always wins). <b>Facts</b> (<code>ansible_*</code>) are gathered per host at run start — CPU count, OS, IPs, NUMA — and drive templates and conditionals.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>group_vars/compute.yml   →  slurm_role: compute\nhost_vars/gpu003.yml     →  gpu_count: 8\n-e "slurm_role=drain"    →  overrides everything for this run</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Company dress code: firm-wide default, then department override, then team, then your manager\'s note for today. A direct instruction from the CEO for one meeting (<code>-e</code>) beats all of it — for that meeting only.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Ansible variable precedence', 'https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_variables.html#variable-precedence-where-should-i-put-a-variable', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Idempotency</b> is the property that running the playbook again changes nothing (and reports no <code>changed</code>). Modules like <code>package</code>, <code>user</code>, <code>copy</code>, <code>template</code>, <code>systemd</code> are idempotent by design — they check state first. The traps are <code>command</code>/<code>shell</code>, which always run and always report changed unless you guard them:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>- name: initialise the DB once\n  command: /opt/app/db-init\n  args: { creates: /var/lib/app/.initialised }   # skip if this exists\n  # or: register a check, then \'when:\' on its result\n  # or: changed_when / failed_when to define truth yourself</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A door that "should be locked" vs the instruction "turn the key clockwise". The first is safe to repeat; the second, repeated, unlocks a locked door. <code>shell:</code> tasks are the second kind until you add a <code>creates:</code> or a <code>when:</code>.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: is this task idempotent?', '../ansible/step2_idempotency_check.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Handlers</b> run once at the end of a play, only if notified — "config changed, so restart the service" without restarting when nothing changed. For fleet changes you never want all hosts at once: <code>serial: "10%"</code> (or a list like <code>[1, 10, 50%]</code>) rolls the change in waves; <code>max_fail_percentage</code> aborts if a wave goes bad; a health-check task with <code>until</code>/<code>retries</code> gates the next wave; <code>run_once</code> + <code>delegate_to</code> drains a scheduler node before touching it.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>- hosts: compute\n  serial: "20%"\n  max_fail_percentage: 5\n  tasks:\n    - command: scontrol update nodename={{ inventory_hostname }} state=drain reason=patch\n    - import_role: { name: os_patch }\n    - reboot:\n    - uri: { url: "http://localhost:9100/metrics" }   # node exporter back?\n      retries: 30\n      delay: 10</code></pre>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: rolling update with health gates', '../ansible/step3_rolling_update.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: at 2,000 hosts, Ansible\'s defaults are the bottleneck. Raise <code>forks</code> (default 5 → 50–100); switch <code>strategy: free</code> so fast hosts do not wait on slow ones at every task; enable <b>pipelining</b> and <b>SSH ControlPersist</b> (fewer connections); <b>fact-cache</b> to Redis/jsonfile and gather only what you use (<code>gather_subset</code>); prefer <b>loops over many tasks</b> and <b>package: name=[list]</b> over per-package tasks. Structure with <b>roles</b> and <b>collections</b>; test with <b>molecule</b>; run from <b>AWX/AAP</b> or a CI runner with a locked <code>requirements.yml</code>. Push-based Ansible + image baking (Module 7) is the standard cluster combo: image for the base, Ansible for the last mile and day-2.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"The playbook takes 3 hours" is almost always: <code>forks: 5</code>, full fact gathering on every run, linear strategy, and 40 separate <code>yum install</code> tasks. Fixing those four routinely turns 3 hours into 15 minutes.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Ansible performance tuning', 'https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_strategies.html', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Automation at fleet scale, where the failure modes are operational, not syntactic.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — the playbook is "not safe to re-run"</span><p>Every <code>site.yml</code> run reports 60 <code>changed</code> tasks when nothing changed, so nobody trusts <code>--check</code>. Audit: <code>grep -rEn \'shell:|command:\' roles/ | grep -v \'creates:|changed_when:\'</code>. Fixes: every <code>command</code> gets <code>creates:</code>/<code>changed_when:</code>; replace stacks of <code>lineinfile</code> with one <code>template:</code>; delete <code>ignore_errors: true</code>; add a molecule test that runs converge <b>twice</b> and fails on any second-run <code>changed</code>. Then a clean run is <code>ok</code> everywhere and the team schedules it.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — patch 2,000 nodes in a 4-hour window, jobs running</span><p><code>serial: [1, "5%", "20%"]</code> (canary then widening waves), <code>order: shuffle</code>, <code>max_fail_percentage: 3</code>. Per node: <code>scontrol update state=drain</code> → <code>until</code> <code>squeue</code> shows it idle (up to 2h grace) → <code>dnf security</code> → reboot → NHC health check → <code>state=resume</code>. Resumable via the <code>.retry</code> file; fact-cache so re-runs are fast. The canary of <code>1</code> with <code>max_fail_percentage: 3</code> aborts on <i>any</i> canary failure — exactly right.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Two rules that bite</b>: <code>-e</code> extra-vars beat <b>everything</b> in the repo (grep CI/cron for it); and an <code>sshd</code> handler must use <code>template: validate: /usr/sbin/sshd -t -f %s</code> + <code>reloaded</code> (not <code>restart</code>) or one typo locks out the fleet. <code>strategy: free</code> alone was ~40% of one team\'s 3h→12min speedup.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: idempotency audit, rolling patch, var precedence, speed', '../ansible/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Which variable source wins: host_vars, group_vars, or -e extra-vars?', opts: ['group_vars always wins', 'host_vars always wins', 'Command-line -e extra-vars has the highest precedence and overrides both', 'Whichever loads last alphabetically'], ok: 2, why: 'Precedence (low→high): role defaults < group_vars/all < group_vars/group < host_vars < play vars < extra-vars. `-e` is the intentional final override.' },
    { q: 'Why is a bare `shell:` or `command:` task usually not idempotent, and how do you fix it?', opts: ['It is always idempotent', 'It runs every time and reports changed; guard it with creates:/removes:, a when: on a registered check, or changed_when', 'You cannot fix it — never use shell', 'Add ignore_errors: true'], ok: 1, why: 'Ad-hoc commands have no state model, so Ansible cannot tell if work is needed. `creates:`, `when:`, and `changed_when:` let you define "already done" so re-runs are safe and honest.' },
    { q: 'You must patch 2,000 compute nodes without an all-at-once outage. Best mechanism?', opts: ['Run the playbook 2,000 times by hand', 'serial: with waves + max_fail_percentage + an until/retries health check gating each wave (drain, patch, reboot, verify)', 'Set forks: 2000 and go', 'ignore_errors on every task'], ok: 1, why: '`serial` rolls the change in controlled batches; `max_fail_percentage` stops the roll on trouble; a retrying health check ensures each batch is healthy before the next. That is a safe fleet update.' } ] },

/* ============================ 07 · WAREWULF ============================ */
{ num: 7, emoji: '🥾', title: 'Warewulf Provisioning', layer: 'Automation',
  tagline: 'Booting a thousand diskless nodes from one image: the DHCP→TFTP→iPXE→kernel handshake, container images as node images, and overlays.',
  apps: ['🖧 standing up a new cluster', '🔁 "reboot fixes it" instead of a rebuild', '🧬 identical nodes, guaranteed'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p><b>Warewulf</b> provisions cluster nodes over the network: no per-node OS install, no local disk required. The controller holds one <b>node image</b> and a set of <b>overlays</b>; each node PXE-boots, pulls the image into RAM (or to disk if you want stateful), applies its overlays, and is running the OS in a couple of minutes. Add a node = plug it in and register its MAC.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A hotel where every room is made up from one master template each morning, plus a small card with that room\'s number and preferences. Rooms never "drift" — a messy room is fixed by re-making it, not by cleaning around the mess.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>On a Warewulf cluster, a compute node with a corrupted filesystem or a bad manual change is fixed by <code>ipmitool ... chassis power cycle</code> — it comes back byte-identical to its siblings. There is nothing to "repair".</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: the PXE boot handshake, packet by packet', '../warewulf/step1_pxe_handshake.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The network boot chain, in order: node NIC sends a <b>DHCP DISCOVER</b> → Warewulf\'s DHCP replies with an IP <i>and</i> <code>next-server</code> + a boot filename → node fetches that file over <b>TFTP</b> (small, simple) — this is <b>iPXE</b> → iPXE is smarter, so it fetches the <b>kernel</b> and <b>initramfs</b> over <b>HTTP</b> (fast) → the kernel starts, the Warewulf init in the initramfs pulls the <b>node image</b> and <b>overlays</b> over HTTP → pivot to the real root → systemd. UEFI + SecureBoot adds a signed shim in front.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>DHCP  →  "you are 10.0.1.53, boot from 10.0.0.1 : undionly.kpxe"\nTFTP  →  iPXE binary\nHTTP  →  vmlinuz + initramfs  (+ kernel args: wwid, ww4.node=...)\nHTTP  →  container image rootfs  +  system/runtime overlays\n         → switch_root → /sbin/init</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Asking for directions at the border (DHCP), being handed a basic paper map to the depot (TFTP/iPXE), then at the depot getting a fast car and the full GPS route (HTTP kernel), which drives you to the furnished house (image + overlays).</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Warewulf docs', 'https://warewulf.org/docs/', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Warewulf 4\'s node images <b>are OCI container images</b>. You <code>wwctl container import docker://rockylinux:9</code>, chroot in to add Slurm, the Lustre client, MPI, monitoring — or better, build it with a <b>Containerfile</b> in CI — then <code>wwctl container build</code> bakes the bootable image. This means your node OS is versioned, diffable, rebuildable, and can be tested in a container before a single node reboots.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Kernel upgrade on 1,000 nodes: build image <code>v2026.09</code>, boot it on one canary node, run the acceptance benchmarks from Module 4, then <code>wwctl node set --container v2026.09 compute[001-1000]</code> and roll reboots in waves. Rollback is setting the tag back.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: diff two node images', '../warewulf/step3_image_diff.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Overlays</b> are how identical nodes get their small differences. A <b>system overlay</b> is applied at build/provision time and baked in (sshd host keys strategy, <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>, base network). A <b>runtime overlay</b> is fetched and refreshed <i>while the node runs</i> (via <code>wwctl overlay build</code> + a periodic pull) — good for things that change without a reboot: <code>/etc/hosts</code>, <code>/etc/slurm/</code>, munge key, user list. Overlays are Go <b>templates</b>: <code>{{ .Ipaddr }}</code>, <code>{{ .Hostname }}</code>, <code>{{ range .Tags }}</code>, so one file renders per-node.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>The master uniform (image) vs the name badge and today\'s shift card (overlays). The uniform changes once a season; the badge is printed per person; the shift card is swapped daily without a new uniform.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: render a per-node overlay template', '../warewulf/step2_overlay_render.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: the payoff of stateless provisioning is a <b>fleet with no snowflakes</b> — drift is structurally impossible, so debugging is "compare to a sibling" and recovery is a reboot. Operational care: keep the controller HA (it is now in the boot path for the whole cluster — though booted nodes survive a controller outage); segment the provisioning VLAN; watch TFTP/HTTP load on mass reboot (stagger, or use a caching tier / multiple controllers); size node RAM for the in-RAM rootfs (tmpfs) or go hybrid; and pair it with Ansible (Module 6) for day-2 config that should not need an image rebuild. Compare with alternatives: xCAT (IBM heritage, heavier), Bright/BCM (commercial), MAAS (cloud-style), Foreman/Katello (stateful + Puppet).</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Mass reboot of 2,000 nodes after a power event can DDoS your own provisioning server. Fixes: <code>wwctl</code> multiple controllers, HTTP caching proxies per rack, or a boot-storm delay in the kernel args so nodes wake in a staggered ramp.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Warewulf on GitHub', 'https://github.com/warewulf/warewulf', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Stateless provisioning when a thousand nodes boot at once.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — a node will not PXE boot</span><p>Diagnose in boot order: is it reaching DHCP? (<code>tcpdump -ni provision0 port 67 or 68 -e</code>, <code>wwctl node list -a</code> — MAC registered? right <code>--netdev</code>? a rogue DHCP server on the VLAN?). Is DHCP offering <code>next-server</code> + <code>filename</code> (options 66/67)? Is TFTP serving the iPXE binary (UEFI vs BIOS mismatch)? Is HTTP on :9873 reachable through the firewall? Kernel panic "no root" → the image was never built or an overlay errored.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — boot storm after a datacenter power event</span><p>1,800 nodes PXE-boot at once; the controller\'s <code>httpd</code> pegs, TFTP times out, failed nodes retry and make it worse. <b>Immediate</b>: power the racks on in staggered groups (<code>ipmitool ... power on</code> in batches of ~50 with a sleep). <b>Permanent</b>: multiple Warewulf controllers with DHCP failover, a per-rack caching HTTP proxy for the image/kernel objects, a boot delay keyed off the last IP octet, and the image store on local NVMe not NFS.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>"Works on cpu001 but not cpu002" on identical nodes</b> should be impossible on a stateless fleet — so it is an overlay-template bug that renders differently for that node, a hardware difference (dead NIC port, old BMC firmware), or a runtime overlay that did not refresh. <code>wwctl overlay show runtime --render cpu002</code> prints exactly what that node gets — diff it against its sibling.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: PXE debugging, boot storms, fleet kernel upgrade, tmpfs RAM', '../warewulf/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Put the network-boot chain in order.', opts: ['HTTP → TFTP → DHCP → kernel', 'DHCP (IP + boot file) → TFTP (iPXE) → HTTP (kernel + initramfs) → HTTP (image + overlays) → init', 'PXE → NFS → GRUB → login', 'DNS → DHCP → SSH → image'], ok: 1, why: 'The NIC gets an address and a boot filename from DHCP, pulls iPXE over TFTP, then iPXE uses fast HTTP for the kernel/initramfs and the Warewulf init fetches the image and overlays before pivoting to the real root.' },
    { q: 'In Warewulf 4, what is a compute node\'s OS image?', opts: ['A hand-installed RPM set per node', 'An OCI/OCI-compatible container image, built and versioned like any container, then baked into a bootable image', 'A tarball of /etc', 'A VM disk file'], ok: 1, why: 'Warewulf 4 uses container images as node images — import or build with a Containerfile, test in a container, roll to nodes by changing the assigned tag, roll back by changing it back.' },
    { q: 'You need to update /etc/slurm/slurm.conf and the munge key across all nodes without rebooting. Use:', opts: ['A new node image and a full reboot cycle', 'A runtime overlay — rebuilt and re-pulled on running nodes', 'Manual scp to every node', 'A system overlay only'], ok: 1, why: 'Runtime overlays are designed for files that change on live nodes. System overlays are baked at provision time and would need a re-provision/reboot to change.' } ] },

/* ============================ 08 · HPC / SLURM ============================ */
{ num: 8, emoji: '🖥️', title: 'HPC Cluster Architecture & Slurm', layer: 'HPC',
  tagline: 'Turning a room of servers into a shared supercomputer: partitions and QOS, the backfill scheduler, fairshare, MPI ranks, and process binding.',
  apps: ['🧑‍🔬 shared research clusters', '📅 "why is my job still pending?"', '⚖️ fair use across teams'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>A cluster has roles: <b>login</b> nodes (you SSH here, edit, submit — never compute), <b>compute</b> nodes (the muscle), a <b>scheduler/controller</b>, shared <b>storage</b> (Module 10), and a fast <b>interconnect</b> (Module 9). A <b>batch scheduler</b> — usually <b>Slurm</b> — is the operating system of the whole thing: you describe a job (nodes, cores, memory, time, partition), it queues, and Slurm runs it when the resources are free and it is your turn.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A hospital operating-theatre bookings desk. Surgeons (users) request a room, a team, and 2 hours. The desk (Slurm) sequences everyone by urgency, fairness, and what fits the gaps — you do not walk in and grab a theatre.</p></div>' +
    '<pre><code>#!/bin/bash\n#SBATCH -p compute -N 4 --ntasks-per-node=48 -t 02:00:00\n#SBATCH --mem=0            # all memory on the node\nsrun ./my_mpi_app          # srun launches ranks under Slurm</code></pre>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: the backfill scheduler', '../hpc/step1_slurm_backfill.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Partitions</b> are queues over node sets (<code>debug</code>, <code>compute</code>, <code>gpu</code>, <code>largemem</code>) with limits (max walltime, max nodes/user). <b>QOS</b> layers priority, preemption and limits on top. The scheduler does two passes: <b>priority</b> ordering, then <b>backfill</b> — start lower-priority jobs early <i>if</i> they finish before the top job\'s resources are needed. That is why an accurate <code>-t</code> (time limit) helps you: a short job slots into gaps; a "24:00:00 just in case" job never backfills.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Boarding a plane: first class first (priority), but the gate agent waves a quick standby passenger into an empty seat if they will be seated before the group that seat is held for (backfill). Tell the truth about your carry-on and you board sooner.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Slurm scheduling & backfill', 'https://slurm.schedmd.com/sched_config.html', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Fairshare</b> keeps a shared machine fair: each account gets a target share of the cluster; recent usage <b>decays</b> over a half-life; your job priority rises when you are under your share and falls when you are over. Total priority is a weighted sum: <code>age + fairshare + partition + QOS + job size + TRES</code> (see <code>sprio</code>). Preemption (QOS-based, or suspend/requeue) lets a high-priority job reclaim nodes from a low-priority one.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"My colleague\'s jobs always start before mine" is usually fairshare working correctly: their account ran little last week, yours ran a lot, so the scheduler is rebalancing. It equalises over the half-life, not per-job.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: topology-aware placement', '../hpc/step3_topology_placement.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Parallel jobs use <b>MPI</b>: N <b>ranks</b> (processes), each with an ID, communicating by messages. <b>Point-to-point</b> (<code>MPI_Send/Recv</code>) and <b>collectives</b> (<code>MPI_Bcast</code>, <code>MPI_Allreduce</code>, <code>MPI_Alltoall</code>) — the collectives are where scaling dies, because <code>Allreduce</code> cost grows with rank count and rides the network (Module 9). <b>PMIx</b> is how Slurm and the MPI runtime bootstrap ranks fast at large scale. Binding matters: <code>--cpu-bind=cores --distribution=block:block</code> and correct <code>OMP_NUM_THREADS</code> so ranks and threads land on the right cores/NUMA node (Module 12) — bad binding silently halves performance.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>srun --mpi=pmix -n 192 --cpu-bind=cores \\\n     --hint=nomultithread ./solver     # 4 nodes x 48 cores</code></pre>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: an MPI ring / Allreduce cost model', '../hpc/step2_mpi_ring.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: the control plane must not fall over. <b>slurmctld</b> (HA with a backup + shared state dir), <b>slurmdbd</b> + MySQL/MariaDB for accounting (<code>sacct</code>, fairshare history), <b>munge</b> for auth (clock skew &gt; a few minutes breaks everything — run NTP/chrony), and <b>slurmd</b> per node with cgroup plugins for real resource enforcement. Operate it: <code>scontrol update nodename=... state=drain</code> before maintenance, <code>reason=</code> always; watch for nodes in <code>drain</code>/<code>down</code> with <code>sinfo -R</code>; prologue/epilogue scripts for health checks (bad node → auto-drain) and cleanup (kill stray procs, drop caches). Node Health Check (NHC) is the standard tool. Alternatives: PBS Pro/OpenPBS, LSF, Grid Engine — Slurm is the de-facto default in research HPC.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>A whole partition goes <code>down</code> at once → check munge and time sync first (chrony), then the controller\'s disk (state save dir full), then the network to the nodes. It is almost never the nodes themselves.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Slurm quickstart', 'https://slurm.schedmd.com/quickstart.html', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Running the scheduler when users and hardware fight it.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — "my job has been PENDING for two days"</span><p>The <code>(Reason)</code> column is the whole answer: <code>Priority</code> (others ahead — check <code>sprio</code>, you may be over fairshare), <code>Resources</code> (<code>scontrol show job</code> <code>StartTime</code> is the backfill estimate), <code>ReqNodeNotAvail</code> (you asked for a feature whose nodes are drained — <code>sinfo -R</code>), a QOS/account cap, or <code>--time</code> over the partition max. The usual fix: a smaller/shorter job backfills. <b>Over-requesting walltime is the #1 self-inflicted delay.</b></p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — an entire partition goes DOWN in one minute</span><p>A synchronised failure ⇒ a shared cause. Check shared things first: <code>munge -n | unmunge</code> (key mismatch), <code>chronyc tracking</code> (clock skew &gt; ~5 min breaks munge), <code>StateSaveLocation</code> disk full, <code>slurmctld</code> OOM/crashed with no backup, a management-switch reboot. Individual hardware faults do not synchronise.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Nodes drain themselves mid-job</b> (<code>sinfo -R</code>: <code>Low RealMemory</code>, <code>Kill task failed</code>, <code>NHC: ib0 down</code>) — the epilogue/NHC found a fault and kept new jobs off. <code>Kill task failed</code> = a job\'s processes would not die (D-state on hung storage) within <code>UnkillableStepTimeout</code>. The config that actually confines a job to its cores + memory: <code>SelectType=select/cons_tres</code> + <code>TaskPlugin=task/cgroup</code> — without it, <code>--mem</code> and <code>-c</code> are advisory.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: pending reasons, partition-down triage, binding, fairshare, slurm.conf', '../hpc/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why does an accurate, shorter --time limit help your job start sooner?', opts: ['Slurm charges by requested time', 'Backfill can start your job early only if it provably finishes before the resources are needed for the top-priority job — a tight time limit fits more gaps', 'Shorter jobs get higher base priority always', 'It does not matter'], ok: 1, why: 'The backfill pass needs your end time to guarantee it will not delay the highest-priority job. Over-requesting walltime makes your job ineligible for those gaps.' },
    { q: 'What does fairshare do?', opts: ['Splits each job evenly across nodes', 'Adjusts job priority based on an account\'s recent usage vs its target share, with time decay, so the machine balances between groups over a half-life', 'Guarantees every user the same number of nodes at all times', 'Charges users money'], ok: 1, why: 'Fairshare is a priority factor: under-served accounts float up, heavy recent users sink, and usage decays so it self-corrects over the configured half-life rather than instantly.' },
    { q: 'An entire Slurm partition shows state=down. First things to check?', opts: ['Rebuild every node image', 'munge auth + time synchronization (chrony), then the controller state-save disk and network to the nodes — a fleet-wide failure is usually control-plane, not hardware', 'Replace the InfiniBand cables', 'Resubmit the jobs'], ok: 1, why: 'Simultaneous, fleet-wide node failure points at something shared: munge key/clock skew, controller disk full, or a management-network break. Individual hardware faults do not synchronise.' } ] },

/* ============================ 09 · FABRIC ============================ */
{ num: 9, emoji: '🔌', title: 'Networking & RDMA Fabric', layer: 'HPC',
  tagline: 'The wires that make MPI fast: RDMA vs the kernel TCP path, InfiniBand vs RoCE, fat-tree bisection bandwidth, the subnet manager, and diagnostics.',
  apps: ['⚡ tightly-coupled MPI jobs', '🧵 "collectives got slow at 128 nodes"', '🏗️ cluster network design'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>Normal networking copies data kernel→app, app→kernel, through the TCP stack, with an interrupt and a CPU on both ends. <b>RDMA</b> (Remote Direct Memory Access) lets one node\'s NIC write straight into another node\'s memory — <b>kernel bypass</b>, <b>zero copy</b>, almost no CPU. That is how MPI hits ~1 µs latency and near-line-rate bandwidth. The two flavours: <b>InfiniBand</b> (a purpose-built fabric) and <b>RoCEv2</b> (RDMA over Ethernet/UDP, needs a lossless DCB config).</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Kernel TCP is posting a letter: envelope, sorting office, postman, recipient opens it. RDMA is a pneumatic tube that drops the message directly onto the recipient\'s desk while they keep working. Same building, wildly different latency.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p><code>ib_write_lat</code> intra-rack should read ~1 µs and <code>ib_write_bw</code> should approach the port rate (e.g. ~24 GB/s on HDR200). If OSU pingpong shows 30 µs, the job fell back to the TCP path — wrong UCX/MPI transport, or a down link.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: RDMA vs TCP cost model', '../fabric/step3_rdma_vs_tcp.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Big clusters wire nodes in a <b>fat-tree</b> (a.k.a. folded Clos): leaf switches at the racks, spine switches above, more bandwidth as you go up so any node can talk to any node at (near) full rate. The key metric is <b>bisection bandwidth</b>: cut the machine in half — how much bandwidth crosses the cut? A <b>full fat-tree</b> is non-blocking (1:1); most clusters are <b>oversubscribed</b> (2:1 or 3:1 at the leaf) to save switch ports and cost, betting that not everyone talks across the cut at once.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>216 nodes, 36-port leaf switches\n1:1  →  18 down : 18 up  per leaf  (non-blocking, most $$)\n2:1  →  24 down : 12 up            (cheaper, fine for many workloads)\n3:1  →  27 down :  9 up            (risky for all-to-all heavy codes)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A motorway network: local roads (leaf) feed on-ramps to the highway (spine). 1:1 means every car can be on the highway at once; 3:1 means rush hour is fine only if most trips stay local.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: compute bisection bandwidth', '../fabric/step2_fat_tree_bisection.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>InfiniBand has no distributed routing protocol — a <b>Subnet Manager</b> (SM: <code>opensm</code> or switch-embedded) discovers the topology, assigns <b>LIDs</b>, and programs every switch\'s forwarding tables. One SM is active, others standby. It also handles <b>partitions</b> (pkeys, like VLANs) and, with the right hardware, <b>adaptive routing</b> (spread flows over spine links) and <b>SHARP</b> (do <code>MPI_Allreduce</code> reductions <i>in the switch</i>, cutting collective latency). RoCE instead relies on Ethernet: <b>PFC</b> + <b>ECN</b>/DCQCN to stay lossless, and it is unforgiving to misconfigure.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>If the SM dies and no standby takes over, existing routes keep working but nothing new comes up cleanly — a rebooted node cannot get a LID. Always run a redundant SM and monitor which one is master (<code>sminfo</code>).</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 InfiniBand architecture primer', 'https://network.nvidia.com/pdf/whitepapers/IB_Intro_WP_190.pdf', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Subnet design and addressing still matter for the <b>management</b> and <b>storage</b> networks (Ethernet). You will plan VLANs and subnets: provisioning, IPMI/BMC, in-band mgmt, storage, and sometimes a separate IPoIB range. CIDR math in your head — a <code>/22</code> is 1,022 usable hosts, a <code>/26</code> is 62 — and a clean scheme (rack in the third octet, node in the fourth) makes <code>ansible</code> inventories and firewall rules write themselves.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>10.aa.rr.nn   aa=network role  rr=rack  nn=node\n10.10.0.0/16  provisioning     10.20.0.0/16  IPMI\n10.30.0.0/16  storage          10.40.0.0/16  IPoIB</code></pre>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: plan the cluster subnets', '../fabric/step1_subnet_plan.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: fabric problems are the hardest to see because they hide as "the app is slow". Toolkit: <code>ibstat</code>/<code>ibportstate</code> (link up? right speed/width — a HDR link negotiated to EDR is a bad cable), <code>ibdiagnet</code> (topology, credit loops, bad SLs), <code>perfquery</code>/<code>ibqueryerrors</code> (<b>SymbolErrors</b>, <b>PortXmitDiscards</b> = congestion, <b>LinkDowned</b>), and OSU/IMB for end-to-end. Watch for: one flaky link poisoning many routes (adaptive routing helps, static routing does not), MTU mismatch on RoCE, <code>PortXmitWait</code> climbing under all-to-all (oversubscription showing its teeth), and firmware skew across HCAs. Baseline the fabric at acceptance (Module 4) and re-run after any physical work.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Classic: a 512-rank job runs fine most days, 3× slower some days. Cause: adaptive routing steering some runs across a marginal spine link with rising <code>SymbolErrors</code>. <code>ibqueryerrors</code> finds it; replacing one cable fixes it.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 OSU Micro-Benchmarks', 'https://mvapich.cse.ohio-state.edu/benchmarks/', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The fabric fails in ways that look like flaky software.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — "the job is slow on some days, fine on others"</span><p>A 512-rank job\'s wall time swings 1.5–3× with no code change. <code>ibqueryerrors -s SymbolErrorCounter,PortXmitDiscards</code> and <code>ibdiagnet --get_phy_info</code> find one marginal cable/transceiver on a spine link with a rising bit-error rate. <b>Adaptive routing</b> spreads flows across all spine links, so runs that route heavy collective traffic over the bad link are slow and runs that do not are fine. Replace the part, <code>ibclearcounters</code>, re-baseline with OSU.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — MPI silently fell back to TCP (10× slow)</span><p><code>osu_latency</code> reads ~25 µs instead of ~1 µs. Check: <code>ibstat</code> (<code>State: Active</code>? rate as expected?), <code>UCX_LOG_LEVEL=info</code> to see the transport picked, <code>ucx_info -d</code>. Causes: HCA link down; <code>UCX_TLS</code> excludes <code>rc</code>/<code>dc</code> or <code>btl</code> forced to <code>tcp,self</code>; no <code>rdma-core</code> in the container image; <code>MEMLOCK</code> ulimit too low so RDMA cannot pin memory; or the subnet manager is not running so the port never went <code>Active</code>.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>RoCEv2 needs a lossless Ethernet</b>: PFC on the RoCE priority only, ECN + DCQCN so senders slow down <i>before</i> PFC pauses — misconfigure it and all-to-all collapses the whole fabric with PFC storms and head-of-line blocking. This is why InfiniBand is often chosen despite the cost: lossless is built in. <b>Oversubscription is a bet on locality</b> — 2:1 is fine if the scheduler keeps jobs in a rack group; pay for 1:1 if jobs routinely span the machine with all-to-all.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: marginal links, TCP fallback, RoCE PFC/ECN, SM failover, fabric design', '../fabric/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'What makes RDMA faster than the normal TCP/IP path for MPI?', opts: ['It compresses packets', 'Kernel bypass + zero-copy: the NIC writes directly into remote memory with almost no CPU involvement or stack traversal, giving ~1 µs latency', 'It uses a faster cable only', 'It skips error checking entirely'], ok: 1, why: 'RDMA removes the copies, the context switches, the interrupt-per-packet and the TCP stack. The NIC does the transfer to registered memory regions directly — that is where the latency and CPU savings come from.' },
    { q: 'A cluster is wired 3:1 oversubscribed at the leaf. Which workload is most at risk?', opts: ['Embarrassingly parallel jobs with no communication', 'Communication-heavy codes doing global all-to-all / large Allreduce across many racks', 'Single-node jobs', 'I/O-bound jobs hitting local disk'], ok: 1, why: 'Oversubscription only bites when many flows cross the leaf-to-spine cut simultaneously. All-to-all and big collectives do exactly that; local or embarrassingly parallel work does not notice.' },
    { q: 'In an InfiniBand fabric, what does the Subnet Manager do and why need a standby?', opts: ['It runs the MPI jobs', 'It discovers topology, assigns LIDs and programs switch forwarding tables; without an active SM, new/rebooted nodes cannot join the fabric', 'It stores files', 'It is only for RoCE'], ok: 1, why: 'IB has no distributed routing — the SM centrally computes routes. If it is down with no standby, established traffic persists but nothing new comes up; redundant SMs are standard.' } ] },

/* ============================ 10 · STORAGE ============================ */
{ num: 10, emoji: '💾', title: 'Parallel Storage', layer: 'HPC',
  tagline: 'Feeding thousands of ranks from one filesystem: MDS/OSS/OST anatomy, striping, the metadata bottleneck, and why small random I/O is the enemy.',
  apps: ['🗄️ /scratch on every cluster', '🐢 "the filesystem is slow for everyone"', '💥 the ls that hung the login node'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>One NFS server cannot feed 2,000 nodes. A <b>parallel filesystem</b> (<b>Lustre</b>, <b>BeeGFS</b>, IBM Storage Scale/GPFS, DAOS, VAST) spreads a single namespace across many storage servers so aggregate bandwidth scales with hardware. Clients talk to all servers at once; a big file is <b>striped</b> across many disks and read in parallel.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A library checkout with 50 counters instead of 1. Everyone still sees one catalogue (the namespace), but the actual books come from whichever counters hold your chapters — 50× the throughput at the door.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p><code>/home</code> is usually small, backed-up NFS. <code>/scratch</code> is the big fast Lustre/BeeGFS mount with no backup and a purge policy — you stage data in, run, and copy results out. Treating <code>/scratch</code> as permanent is how people lose a month of runs.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: choose a Lustre stripe layout', '../storage/step1_lustre_stripe.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Lustre anatomy: <b>MDS/MDT</b> (metadata server/target — the directory tree, names, permissions, and <i>where the stripes live</i>), <b>OSS/OST</b> (object storage servers/targets — the actual file data), and a <b>client</b> that presents a POSIX mount. Open a file → ask the MDS → get the <b>layout</b> (which OSTs, stripe size) → then I/O goes straight client↔OSS. <b>Stripe count</b> = how many OSTs a file spans; <b>stripe size</b> = bytes per OST before wrapping.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>lfs setstripe -c 8 -S 4M bigfile      # 8 OSTs, 4 MiB chunks\nlfs getstripe bigfile\n# rule of thumb: 1 GB file → few stripes; 1 TB shared file → 16–48</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>The MDS is the library index desk; the OSTs are the stacks. You ask the desk once where a book\'s chapters are shelved, then walk straight to those aisles. If everyone queues at the one index desk (metadata-heavy workload), the stacks sit idle and the line goes out the door.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Lustre operations manual', 'https://doc.lustre.org/lustre_manual.xhtml', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The number-one parallel-FS outage: a <b>metadata storm</b>. Thousands of ranks each doing <code>open</code>+<code>stat</code>+<code>create</code> on many small files, or a recursive <code>ls -l</code> / <code>find</code> / <code>du</code> over millions of files on a login node, saturates the single MDS. Symptom: every user\'s <code>ls</code> hangs for 30 s, jobs stall in I/O wait (Module 1 D-state), the machine "feels down". Data bandwidth is fine — it is the index desk that is on fire.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Real fix menu: teach users to not <code>ls -l</code> a 5M-file directory (colour <code>ls</code> stats every entry!), use <code>lfs find</code> not <code>find</code>, pack many small files into archives or use <b>MPI-IO</b> / a single shared file, add <b>DNE</b> (multiple MDTs) to shard metadata, and set a scratch purge policy so directories never reach 5M files.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: model a metadata storm', '../storage/step3_metadata_storm.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Access pattern is everything.</b> Parallel filesystems love large, aligned, sequential I/O (≥ 1 MiB, aligned to stripe size) and hate small random I/O and <b>false sharing</b> (many ranks writing different offsets of the same stripe → lock contention). Patterns, best to worst: one big shared file with MPI-IO collective buffering → file-per-process (fine at 100s of ranks, a metadata storm at 100,000) → many ranks appending to one log → random 4K updates. <b>IOR</b> is the benchmark that measures all of these; <b>mdtest</b> measures the metadata side.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Moving house with a truck (large sequential) vs a bicycle doing 10,000 trips with one item each (small random). Same total stuff, and the bicycle also has to check the address list before every single trip.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: IOR throughput vs transfer size', '../storage/step2_ior_throughput.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: modern Lustre adds <b>PFL</b> (Progressive File Layout — few stripes for the first MBs, many for large files, automatically), <b>DoM</b> (Data-on-MDT — tiny files live on fast MDT flash, killing the small-file penalty), <b>DNE</b> (distributed metadata), and flash <b>OST pools</b> for hot data. Operate it: watch <code>lfs df</code> for a near-full or imbalanced OST (writes fail or skew), <code>lctl get_param *.*.stats</code>, per-job I/O with the Lustre jobstats + Slurm jobid, and quota (block <i>and</i> inode — inode exhaustion from small files is common). Alternatives trade-offs: BeeGFS is easier to run, GPFS is feature-rich and licensed, DAOS/VAST/WEKA are flash-native and reshape these rules.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"Writes started failing but <code>df</code> shows 20% free" → one OST is 100% full because a job set stripe_count=1 on a 40 TB file. <code>lfs df</code> shows the skew; the fix is a sane default layout and PFL so no single OST gets the whole file.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 IOR / mdtest benchmarks', 'https://github.com/hpc/ior', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Parallel storage: one user can take down everyone.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — <code>ls</code> hangs cluster-wide</span><p>Every user reports <code>ls</code>, tab-completion and job startup hanging 20–60 s; the MDS shows load 300 in <code>ll_md_*</code> threads. <code>lctl get_param mdt.*.job_stats</code> names the Slurm JOBID hammering metadata — usually a <code>du -sh</code> on 30M files from a login node, or 4,000 small-file <code>open/stat</code> × 8,000 ranks. Immediate: <code>scancel</code>/renice the offender. Structural: DNE (add MDTs, <code>lfs setdirstripe</code>), Data-on-MDT for tiny files, login-node cgroup limits, and quotas on <b>inodes</b> not just blocks.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — ENOSPC with <code>df</code> showing 20% free</span><p><code>lfs df</code> per-OST shows <b>one OST at 100%</b>: a job wrote a 40 TB file with <code>stripe_count=1</code>, so it all landed on a single OST. Fix: a default PFL layout (<code>lfs setstripe -E 1G -c 1 -E 100G -c 8 -E -1 -c 24</code>), <code>lfs migrate</code> big objects off the full OST, <code>max_create_count=0</code> to stop new allocations there, and monitoring on per-OST %full with an alert at 85%.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>IOR shows 30 GB/s, the real job gets 2 GB/s</b>: Darshan reveals file-per-process 8 KiB records + an <code>fsync</code> after every write. Fix with MPI-IO collective writes (<code>romio_cb_write=enable</code>, <code>cb_buffer_size=16M</code>), align to stripe size, buffer ≥ 1 MiB in memory, stripe across 16–32 OSTs, <code>fsync</code> once. HDF5/ADIOS2 do most of this for you. <b>Client evictions</b> (<code>-108 ESHUTDOWN</code>) usually trace back to a fabric blip — fix Module 9 first.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: metadata storms, OST imbalance, IOR vs reality, evictions, Lustre vs BeeGFS', '../storage/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'On Lustre, which component is the bottleneck during a "thousands of small files" workload?', opts: ['The OSTs (data)', 'The MDS/MDT (metadata) — open/stat/create all hit it, so the single metadata path saturates while data servers idle', 'The client cache', 'The InfiniBand fabric'], ok: 1, why: 'Small-file and directory-walk workloads are metadata-bound. Data bandwidth is untouched; the MDS is doing all the work. DNE, DoM, and behavioural fixes address it.' },
    { q: 'Why does `ls -l` on a directory with millions of files hang a login node?', opts: ['It reads every file\'s contents', 'It issues a stat() per entry (colour ls also stats), flooding the single metadata server and stalling everyone', 'It sorts in O(n^2)', 'ls has a memory leak'], ok: 1, why: 'A long listing needs metadata for every entry. Millions of stat() calls hammer the MDS; every other user\'s metadata ops queue behind it. `lfs find` and archiving small files are the fixes.' },
    { q: 'Best I/O pattern for a large parallel job writing one dataset from 4,000 ranks?', opts: ['4,000 separate 4 KiB random-write files', 'One shared file via MPI-IO with collective buffering and large, stripe-aligned transfers', 'Each rank appends line-by-line to one shared log', 'Write to /home'], ok: 1, why: 'Parallel filesystems reward large, aligned, sequential I/O and collective buffering. File-per-process explodes metadata at that scale; tiny random writes and shared-append cause lock contention.' } ] },

/* ============================ 11 · CONTAINERS ============================ */
{ num: 11, emoji: '📦', title: 'Containers & Reproducibility', layer: 'HPC',
  tagline: 'Making a run reproduce in five years: Apptainer/Singularity vs Docker, binding host MPI and fabric, Spack concretization, and environment modules.',
  apps: ['🔬 "reproduce the 2021 paper"', '📦 shipping a toolchain to another site', '🧩 dependency hell on a shared cluster'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>On a shared cluster you cannot <code>apt install</code>, you are not root, and 300 users need conflicting versions of everything. Three tools solve different layers: <b>environment modules</b> (Lmod) swap pre-built software into your <code>$PATH</code>; <b>Spack</b> builds from source with every dependency and compiler pinned; <b>Apptainer</b> (formerly Singularity) packages a whole userspace into one file you carry between machines and years.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Modules = the tool cabinet in a shared workshop (pick the drill you want). Spack = a machine that builds you a custom tool from raw stock to your exact spec. Apptainer = shipping the entire workbench, tools bolted down, in a sealed crate.</p></div>' +
    '<pre><code>module load gcc/13.2 openmpi/5.0 fftw/3.3.10\nspack install hdf5@1.14.3 %gcc@13.2 +mpi ^openmpi@5.0\napptainer exec solver_v3.sif ./run.sh</code></pre>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: inside a SIF file', '../containers/step1_apptainer_sif.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Apptainer</b> is Docker rethought for HPC. Key differences: a container is a <b>single <code>.sif</code> file</b> (immutable, signable, sits on Lustre, easy to archive) not a layer store; it runs <b>rootless</b> and <b>you stay yourself</b> inside — UID maps through, so files you create are owned by you, not root; there is <b>no daemon</b>; and <code>$HOME</code>, <code>$PWD</code> and <code>/tmp</code> are bind-mounted by default so it "just works" in a batch job. It can build <i>from</i> Docker images (<code>apptainer build app.sif docker://...</code>).</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Docker is a shipping-container yard with a crane and a foreman (the daemon) stacking layers. Apptainer hands you one welded box you can put on any truck yourself, no crane, no foreman, and it only contains what you own.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Why clusters ban the Docker daemon: <code>docker run</code> effectively grants root (mount the host, add yourself to sudoers). Apptainer\'s rootless, no-privilege-escalation model is what makes user-supplied containers safe on a shared machine.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Apptainer docs', 'https://apptainer.org/docs/user/latest/', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The catch with containers in HPC: <b>MPI and the fabric driver must match the host</b>. A container with its own OpenMPI talking to the host\'s InfiniBand needs the host\'s libraries and the right transport. Two models: <b>bind (hybrid)</b> — mount the host MPI + <code>/dev/infiniband</code> + libfabric/UCX into the container (fast, tightly coupled, fragile across sites); or <b>container MPI matched to host PMI</b> — the container ships a compatible MPI and only the process-manager interface (PMI/PMIx) is shared. GPU is similar: <code>--nv</code> binds the host NVIDIA driver stack.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>apptainer exec --nv \\\n  --bind /opt/mpi,/usr/lib64/libibverbs.so.1,/dev/infiniband \\\n  solver.sif   srun --mpi=pmix -n 384 ./solver</code></pre>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: bind-mount resolution for MPI', '../containers/step3_mpi_bind_mount.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Spack</b> handles "build 200 packages with consistent compilers/flags". Its core trick is <b>concretization</b>: you give an abstract spec (<code>hdf5 +mpi</code>), Spack\'s solver resolves every dependency, version, variant, compiler and target microarchitecture into a fully concrete DAG, then builds it and installs each package in a hashed prefix so incompatible builds coexist. It auto-generates Lmod modules. <b>EasyBuild</b> is the alternative (recipe-driven "easyconfigs", big curated set, strong at reproducible toolchains).</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Concretization is a travel agent turning "somewhere warm in spring, not too expensive" into a specific flight, seat, hotel and transfer that are all mutually consistent and bookable — before anyone leaves.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: concretize a Spack spec', '../containers/step2_spack_concretize.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: reproducibility is <b>layered</b> and you pick the depth the science needs. Level 1: pin module versions in the job script. Level 2: a Spack <b>environment</b> (<code>spack.yaml</code> + <code>spack.lock</code>) committed to git — rebuildable exactly. Level 3: an Apptainer image with a committed <b>definition file</b> and a digest, archived alongside the data. Level 4: image + input data + workflow (Snakemake/Nextflow) + a DOI. Trade-offs: bind-mounting host MPI is fast but couples you to one site; a fully self-contained container is portable but may leave 5–15% performance on the table. For STIG-regulated environments, containers need their own scanning (Module 3) and a trusted base image pipeline.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"Reproduce Figure 4 from our 2021 paper" succeeds when there is a <code>solver_2021.sif</code> + <code>spack.lock</code> + the SLURM script in the repo. It fails when the method section says "we used GCC and OpenMPI" and the cluster has been reimaged twice since.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Spack & reproducible environments', 'https://spack.readthedocs.io/en/latest/environments.html', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Reproducibility and container performance, tested against real requests.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — "reproduce Figure 4 from our 2026 paper" (it is 2031)</span><p><b>Succeeds</b> when the repo has <code>solver_2026.sif</code> (+ its definition file + a recorded digest), a <code>spack.lock</code>, the <code>sbatch</code> script, and the input data (or a DOI). <b>Fails</b> when the methods section says "we used GCC and OpenMPI", the cluster has been reimaged twice, and <code>openmpi/4.0.2</code> no longer exists. The reproducibility ladder: pin module versions → a committed Spack environment (<code>spack.yaml</code> + <code>spack.lock</code>) → an archived Apptainer image + def file → image + data + workflow engine + DOI.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — a containerized MPI job runs 10× slow over InfiniBand</span><p>The MPI/fabric stack <i>inside</i> the container does not match the host <code>libibverbs</code>/UCX, so the transport silently degrades to TCP. Fix with the <b>hybrid model</b>: bind-mount the host MPI + <code>libibverbs</code> + <code>/dev/infiniband</code> + PMI/PMIx, and let <code>srun</code> launch the ranks sharing PMIx. Fully self-contained is portable but leaves 5–15% on the table.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Why HPC uses Apptainer over the Docker daemon</b>: rootless, no daemon (the Docker daemon effectively grants root on a shared login node), your UID maps straight through, and an image is one immutable <code>.sif</code> file that lives happily on parallel storage. For a STIG-regulated site, container images need their <i>own</i> scanning (a container profile, not the host STIG) from a trusted base-image pipeline.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: reproducing old results, MPI-in-container, Apptainer vs Docker', '../containers/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why do HPC clusters prefer Apptainer/Singularity over the Docker daemon?', opts: ['Docker is slower at compute', 'Apptainer runs rootless with no privilege escalation and no daemon, and packages to one immutable SIF file — safe for user-supplied images on a shared multi-user machine', 'Docker cannot run MPI at all', 'Apptainer images are smaller by law'], ok: 1, why: 'Docker\'s daemon model effectively grants root. Apptainer keeps your UID, escalates no privileges, needs no daemon, and its single-file images live happily on parallel storage — the right security and ops fit for HPC.' },
    { q: 'A containerized MPI job falls back to TCP and runs 10x slower over InfiniBand. Likely cause?', opts: ['The SIF file is corrupt', 'The container MPI / fabric libraries do not match the host — you must bind-mount the host MPI + libibverbs + /dev/infiniband, or ship a host-compatible MPI sharing PMI/PMIx', 'Apptainer disables RDMA', 'You need more ranks'], ok: 1, why: 'RDMA needs the container to use libraries compatible with the host verbs/UCX stack. The hybrid model binds host MPI and the IB device in; otherwise the transport silently degrades to sockets.' },
    { q: 'What does Spack "concretization" produce?', opts: ['A compressed tarball', 'A fully resolved dependency DAG — every version, variant, compiler and target arch pinned and mutually consistent — ready to build into hashed prefixes', 'A container image', 'A list of module names only'], ok: 1, why: 'Concretization turns an abstract spec into one concrete, consistent build graph. Hashed install prefixes then let conflicting concretizations coexist, and Lmod modules are generated automatically.' } ] },

/* ============================ 12 · KERNEL & PERF TUNING ============================ */
{ num: 12, emoji: '⚡', title: 'Kernel & Performance Tuning', layer: 'Foundation',
  tagline: 'The last 20%: NUMA locality, cgroups v2 resource control, hugepages and the TLB, C-states and governors, CPU isolation and IRQ affinity.',
  apps: ['🎯 latency-sensitive services', '📈 "the benchmark is 15% below spec"', '🔇 killing jitter on compute nodes'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>Out of the box, Linux is tuned for a laptop: power-saving on, scheduler free to migrate anything anywhere, transparent hugepages on, interrupts wherever. For a compute node or a low-latency service you want the opposite — predictable, pinned, awake. Tuning is making the machine boring on purpose.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A road car vs a race car. The road car adjusts everything for comfort and economy automatically. The race car is stiff, loud, always in the power band, and every setting is fixed for one job — because surprises cost lap time.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p><code>tuned-adm profile hpc-compute</code> (or <code>latency-performance</code>) flips a couple of dozen of these knobs at once. It is the sane starting point; the rest of this module is what it is actually doing and when to go further.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: NUMA-aware placement', '../tuning/step1_numa_placement.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>NUMA</b>: a 2-socket node has two memory controllers. RAM attached to your socket is <b>local</b> (~90 ns); RAM on the other socket is <b>remote</b> (~140 ns) and shares the inter-socket link. A thread on socket 0 hammering memory allocated on socket 1 can lose 30–50%. Fixes: <code>numactl --cpunodebind=0 --membind=0</code>, Slurm <code>--cpu-bind</code> + <code>--mem-bind</code> (Module 8), <code>first-touch</code> allocation (the socket that first writes a page owns it — so initialise data in the same parallel region that uses it), and check with <code>numastat -p &lt;pid&gt;</code> for <code>numa_miss</code>.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>lscpu | grep NUMA           # NUMA node0 CPUs: 0-23,48-71 ...\nnumactl -H                  # node distances, free MB per node\nnumactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 ./app</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Two kitchens in one restaurant. A chef working from the far pantry crosses the floor for every ingredient and collides with the other chef in the doorway. Keep each chef next to their own pantry.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 What every programmer should know about memory', 'https://people.freebsd.org/~lstewart/articles/cpumemory.pdf', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Hugepages</b>: the CPU caches virtual→physical translations in the <b>TLB</b> (a few thousand entries). With 4 KiB pages, a 16 GiB working set needs ~4M translations — constant TLB misses, each a page-table walk. A <b>2 MiB hugepage</b> covers 512× the memory per entry; <b>1 GiB</b> pages more still. <b>Explicit hugepages</b> (<code>vm.nr_hugepages</code>, <code>hugetlbfs</code>, <code>--mca</code> for MPI) are predictable and used for big scientific arrays and databases. <b>Transparent Huge Pages</b> (THP) does it automatically but its background <code>khugepaged</code> compaction causes latency spikes — many HPC and low-latency shops set THP to <code>madvise</code> or <code>never</code>.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Databases (Oracle, Postgres large shared_buffers, JVMs) explicitly recommend hugepages and disabling THP — the THP stalls show up as periodic p99 latency spikes with no obvious cause in the app.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: TLB reach with 4K vs 2M pages', '../tuning/step3_hugepages_tlb.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>cgroups v2</b> is how you carve a node without VMs. <code>cpu.max</code> (hard CPU quota), <code>cpu.weight</code> (proportional share under contention), <code>memory.max</code> / <code>memory.high</code> (hard cap / throttle-and-reclaim), <code>io.max</code> (block-device bandwidth/IOPS), <code>pids.max</code>. Slurm\'s cgroup plugin uses exactly this to confine each job to its allocation — so a job that requests 4 cores physically cannot use 8, and one that leaks memory hits <i>its</i> <code>memory.max</code> and gets OOM-killed alone (Module 1). <code>systemd</code> exposes the same as <code>CPUQuota=</code>, <code>MemoryMax=</code>, <code>IOWeight=</code>.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Fuse boxes per apartment in a building. One tenant overloading their circuit trips <i>their</i> fuse, not the building main. cgroups are per-workload fuse boxes for CPU, RAM and I/O.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: cgroups v2 CPU & memory limits', '../tuning/step2_cgroups_v2.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view — killing <b>jitter</b> on compute nodes (the reason a 512-rank job runs at the speed of its slowest rank): <b>C-states</b> — deep idle states take microseconds to wake; set <code>intel_idle.max_cstate=1</code> or <code>cpupower idle-set -D 0</code> for latency-critical work (costs power). <b>Frequency</b> — <code>cpupower frequency-set -g performance</code>, disable turbo drift if you need determinism. <b>CPU isolation</b> — <code>isolcpus</code>, <code>nohz_full</code>, <code>rcu_nocbs</code> on the app cores; keep the OS, kernel threads and IRQs on a housekeeping core (<code>irqbalance</code> off, manual <code>/proc/irq/*/smp_affinity</code>). <b>Other jitter sources</b>: THP compaction, NUMA balancing (<code>kernel.numa_balancing=0</code> for pinned jobs), timer ticks, RCU callbacks, <code>kswapd</code>. Measure jitter directly with a spin-loop / <code>cyclictest</code>, and always re-run the Module 4 benchmarks before/after so a "tuning" change that regresses is caught.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Node runs STREAM at 88% of spec while its siblings hit 96%. Walk the list: governor stuck on <code>powersave</code> → THP compaction → a stray daemon pinned to a compute core → C6 wakeups → one DIMM at a lower speed. Four of those five are one-line fixes.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Brendan Gregg: Linux performance', 'https://www.brendangregg.com/linuxperf.html', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Tuning incidents where the "fix" was the bug.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — one node runs STREAM at 88% while its siblings hit 96%</span><p>Walk the list cheapest-first: <code>cpupower frequency-info</code> (governor stuck on <code>powersave</code> — image drift), <code>cat .../transparent_hugepage/enabled</code> (<code>always</code> here, <code>never</code> on the rest — <code>khugepaged</code> compaction stealing cycles), <code>ps -eLo psr,comm</code> (a monitoring agent pinned to a compute core), C6 wakeups, one DIMM at a lower speed (<code>dmidecode -t 17</code>). Four of the five are one-line fixes — bake them into the Warewulf image and enforce with a <code>tuned</code> verify.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — after "tuning", the benchmark got worse</span><p>Someone applied <code>latency-performance</code> (<code>idle=poll</code>, turbo pinned, C-states off) to <i>throughput</i> compute nodes. HPL dropped 4%: with C-states disabled and <code>idle=poll</code>, idle siblings burned the power budget and running cores could not turbo as high; the extra heat nudged thermal limits. <b>Latency tuning and throughput tuning are opposites</b> — fixed frequency + isolated cores for tightly-coupled MPI, let turbo + C-states work for embarrassingly-parallel batch.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Method that survives review</b>: change one knob, re-run the Module 4 benchmark on the same nodes with frequency recorded and ≥ 5 runs, keep only what beats the noise band. Pick one owner for the settings (a custom <code>tuned</code> profile that inherits the base) so <code>tuned</code> and your <code>sysctl.d</code> drop-in do not fight. Bake the final baseline into the stateless image and verify on every build.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: slow-node walk, jitter isolation recipe, cgroup enforcement, latency vs throughput', '../tuning/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'A memory-bound thread on socket 0 uses memory allocated on socket 1. Expected impact and fix?', opts: ['No impact; RAM is RAM', 'Remote NUMA access adds ~50% latency and cross-socket link contention; bind CPU and memory to the same node (numactl / --mem-bind) and first-touch the data there', 'It speeds up due to more controllers', 'Only fix is to buy one-socket nodes'], ok: 1, why: 'Remote memory is slower and shares the interconnect. Co-locating threads and their pages (binding + first-touch allocation in the using parallel region) recovers the loss.' },
    { q: 'Why do hugepages help a large-working-set workload?', opts: ['They compress memory', 'Each TLB entry maps 512× (2 MiB) or more memory, so far fewer TLB misses and page-table walks for the same footprint', 'They make RAM faster electrically', 'They disable swapping'], ok: 1, why: 'The TLB has a fixed small number of entries. Bigger pages = more "TLB reach" = fewer misses. THP does this automatically but its compaction can add latency spikes, so latency-sensitive systems often use explicit hugepages and THP=never.' },
    { q: 'On a shared node, a job requested 4 cores and 16 GB but tries to use 8 cores and leak to 40 GB. With Slurm cgroup enforcement, what happens?', opts: ['It takes the whole node down', 'It is confined: it can only schedule on its 4 cores, and hitting its cgroup memory.max triggers an OOM-kill of that job alone', 'Slurm emails the admin and does nothing', 'The extra usage is billed later'], ok: 1, why: 'cgroups v2 caps are per-job: cpuset limits which cores it can run on, and memory.max bounds it — breaching it OOM-kills within that cgroup, protecting other jobs on the node.' } ] },

/* ============================ 13 · LINUX SECURITY ============================ */
{ num: 13, emoji: '🛂', title: 'Linux Security — LSM, seccomp, capabilities', layer: 'Security',
  tagline: 'The kernel guardrails a process cannot argue with: split-root capabilities, a syscall allowlist, SELinux/AppArmor type enforcement, and namespaces as a boundary.',
  apps: ['🐳 every hardened container', '🛡️ "the RCE went nowhere"', '🔒 systemd sandboxing'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>Module 2 was hardening you <i>choose</i> (SSH config, sudo rules). This is the set of kernel mechanisms that <b>enforce</b> a boundary no matter what a process tries — the difference between "we set a strong policy" and "the process literally cannot make that syscall".</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>Hardening locks the doors and windows. This module is the <i>load-bearing walls</i> — the occupant cannot knock through them even if they want to — plus a guard who checks every internal door against a written list.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>When a news story says "attackers got code execution but could not escalate or move laterally", that is usually a stack of these controls doing their job: dropped capabilities + seccomp + SELinux + <code>NoNewPrivileges</code>.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: root, split into ~40 capabilities', '../linux-security/step1_capabilities_split.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Capabilities</b> split the one big "root" privilege into ~40 units. A web server needs <code>CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> to bind :443 — not <code>CAP_SYS_MODULE</code> (load kernel code), <code>CAP_SYS_PTRACE</code> (read other processes), or <code>CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE</code> (ignore file permissions).</p>' +
    '<pre><code># systemd unit\nCapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE\nAmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE\nNoNewPrivileges=yes</code></pre>' +
    '<p><b>Ambient</b> capabilities exist because non-file, non-inheritable caps are <b>dropped on <code>execve()</code></b> — the lab walks that exact capset math. Least privilege here cuts post-exploit reach by ~95%.</p>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: capset transition on execve', '../linux-security/step1_capabilities_split.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>seccomp-bpf</b> is a syscall firewall: the process loads a BPF filter the kernel runs on <i>every</i> syscall, returning ALLOW / ERRNO / KILL. Default-deny, allow the 40–80 the app really uses, and you have removed 80%+ of the ~350-syscall kernel surface — the surface where local-privilege-escalation bugs live.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A bouncer with a guest list for the kitchen door. Staff (the app\'s real syscalls) walk in; anyone else is turned away, and a few names on a "call security" list get tackled on sight (<code>ptrace</code>, <code>init_module</code>, <code>userfaultfd</code>).</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: syscall allowlist vs an exploit trace', '../linux-security/step2_seccomp_filter.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>SELinux / AppArmor</b> (Linux Security Modules) hook every access <i>after</i> the normal permission check and can only further restrict. SELinux is label-based: every process has a <b>domain</b> (<code>httpd_t</code>), every object a <b>type</b> (<code>shadow_t</code>), and access is denied unless a policy <code>allow</code> rule exists. On <code>execve</code> a <b>domain transition</b> confines the service automatically.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Even <b>root</b> in a confined domain cannot read <code>/etc/shadow</code> if the policy does not allow it. That is why <code>setenforce 0</code> is a serious audit finding — it turns every deny into an allow-with-a-log at once.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: type enforcement + domain transition', '../linux-security/step3_selinux_typeenforce.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: <b>namespaces</b> constrain what a process <i>sees</i> (pids, net, mounts, users) and underpin containers, but user namespaces have been a repeated LPE vector — real containment is namespaces + seccomp + LSM + dropped caps <i>together</i>. Above access control sits <b>integrity</b>: Secure Boot, kernel <code>lockdown</code>, signed modules, and <b>IMA/EVM</b> (measure or appraise every file before execution, extend a TPM PCR for remote attestation). <code>systemd-analyze security &lt;unit&gt;</code> scores a service\'s sandboxing 0–10.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>A CI job that "needs <code>--privileged</code>" almost never does — it needs one <code>--cap-add</code>, one device, or a rootless build tool. <code>--privileged</code> removes every control in this module at once.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Deep dive: confined-RCE, seccomp profiles, IMA', '../linux-security/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The whole point of this module, shown as incidents.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🛡️ Scenario — web RCE that goes nowhere</span><p>IDS alerts on a webshell upload; IR expects lateral movement and finds none. The service ran domain <code>httpd_t</code> (SELinux Enforcing), <code>CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE</code>, <code>NoNewPrivileges=yes</code>, <code>SystemCallFilter=@system-service</code> minus <code>@privileged @mount @debug</code>, <code>ProtectSystem=strict</code>. The attacker had code-exec as <code>apache</code> but: <code>read /etc/shadow</code> → SELinux DENY; <code>setuid(0)</code> → EPERM (NoNewPrivileges); <code>ptrace(sshd)</code> → seccomp KILL; <code>init_module</code> → no <code>CAP_SYS_MODULE</code> + lockdown; <code>write /usr/bin/x</code> → EROFS. <b>None of these stops the RCE — together they turn a full compromise into "can deface files under /var/www/uploads and nothing else".</b></p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — <code>audit2allow</code> used as a "make it work" button</span><p>A new app throws SELinux denials; someone runs <code>ausearch -m avc | audit2allow -M myapp &amp;&amp; semodule -i</code> until they stop. A month later a pentest reads <code>/etc/shadow</code> through the app — because it once <i>tried</i> to (a misconfigured library) and the blanket allow re-opened the hole. Right process: triage <b>every</b> denial — relabel the file (<code>semanage fcontext</code> + <code>restorecon</code>), write a narrow reviewed rule, or fix the app; never blanket-allow.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Scenario — a container "needs" <code>--privileged</code>.</b> It almost never does. <code>--privileged</code> drops every capability limit, the seccomp filter, LSM confinement, and the masked <code>/proc</code> paths at once — a host root shell with extra steps. Map the real need to a specific grant: rootless buildkit for image builds; <code>--cap-add SYS_ADMIN</code> + a userns for <code>mount</code> in tests; <code>--cap-add NET_RAW NET_ADMIN</code> for raw packets. Isolate anything genuinely privileged onto a disposable runner, never the shared node. <code>systemd-analyze security &lt;unit&gt;</code> scores what you are missing.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: confined-RCE anatomy, seccomp-profile method, --privileged replacements, IMA attestation', '../linux-security/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why were "ambient" capabilities added to Linux?', opts: ['To make containers faster', 'Non-file, non-inheritable capabilities are dropped on execve(), so a service could not pass a needed cap to a helper it exec\'d without them', 'To replace SELinux', 'To allow unprivileged users to load kernel modules'], ok: 1, why: 'Before ambient caps, setting a capability on a thread and then exec\'ing a non-setuid program silently lost it. Ambient caps (kernel 4.3) survive execve for exactly this case — e.g. systemd AmbientCapabilities=.' },
    { q: 'What does a default-deny seccomp allowlist mainly buy you?', opts: ['Encryption of syscalls', 'It removes ~80% of the ~350-syscall kernel surface — the attack surface where privilege-escalation bugs live — leaving only the syscalls the app actually uses', 'Faster system calls', 'It replaces the need for user accounts'], ok: 1, why: 'seccomp-bpf runs on every syscall and returns ALLOW/ERRNO/KILL. Allowing only the app\'s real 40–80 syscalls means a kernel LPE in some obscure syscall is simply unreachable from that process.' },
    { q: 'A web app gets RCE as the apache user. SELinux is Enforcing, domain httpd_t. Can it read /etc/shadow?', opts: ['Yes — it is running as a privileged service', 'No — there is no allow rule for httpd_t to read shadow_t, and SELinux is checked after DAC and can only further restrict', 'Only if apache is in the wheel group', 'Yes, because /etc/shadow is world-readable'], ok: 1, why: 'Type enforcement denies by default. httpd_t has no allow rule for shadow_t:file read, so the read fails regardless of Unix uid/permissions. This containment is exactly what setenforce 0 throws away.' } ] },

/* ================== 14 · ANOMALY DETECTION ON ENCRYPTED ACTIVITY ================== */
{ num: 14, emoji: '🕵️', title: 'Anomaly Detection on Encrypted Activity', layer: 'Detection',
  tagline: 'When the payload is encrypted, the shape still talks: flow features, TLS client fingerprints, and beaconing rhythm separate normal from malicious with zero decryption.',
  apps: ['🌐 ~95% of traffic is TLS', '📡 malware C2 over HTTPS', '🚨 NDR / Zeek / RITA'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>Deep packet inspection of the payload is over — TLS 1.3, QUIC and DNS-over-HTTPS hide the content. What replaced it is <b>metadata and behaviour analysis</b>, and it works well.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>You cannot hear what people are saying through the wall, but you can still tell a dinner party from a burglary: how many people, how long, how much comes in and out the door, and whether someone leaves on a perfectly regular schedule.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Corporate proxies, Zeek sensors, and EDR/NDR products all score encrypted traffic this way. Cisco even added router telemetry ("Encrypted Traffic Analytics") specifically for it.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: web vs exfil vs C2 from metadata alone', '../encrypted-anomaly/step1_flow_features.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Flow features</b>: TLS frames application data into records, so the <i>sequence of packet sizes and directions</i> leaks the structure of a session. A detector computes bytes up/down and their ratio, packets-per-second, size entropy, inter-arrival mean and coefficient of variation, and the first ~20 packet sizes as a vector.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>web page   : small request  -> burst of MTU responses -> tail\nfile upload: handshake -> long run of MTU client->server packets\nC2 shell   : tiny request &lt;-&gt; small response, seconds apart, repeat</code></pre>' +
    '<p>Exfiltration shows up as sustained upload where that host normally downloads — no payload needed.</p>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: the feature vector, 3 flow types', '../encrypted-anomaly/step1_flow_features.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The TLS <b>ClientHello</b> is chosen by the client\'s <i>library</i>, not the user: ordered cipher suites, extensions, curves, ALPN. Hash that tuple and you get a fingerprint — <b>JA3</b> (MD5 of the CSV) or the newer, GREASE-resilient <b>JA4</b>. A stock browser, <code>curl</code>, Go <code>net/http</code>, and Cobalt Strike all have <i>different</i> fingerprints.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>You cannot read the letter, but the handwriting on the envelope is unmistakable. Everyone in the office uses the company stationery; a ransom-note font is worth a second look.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>On an egress proxy you allowlist the handful of fingerprints your sanctioned software produces and alert on anything else — plus cheap anomalies like SNI ↔ certificate-name mismatch (domain fronting).</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: JA3-style fingerprint + egress allowlist', '../encrypted-anomaly/step2_tls_fingerprint.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Beaconing</b>: implants call home on a timer. Even with jitter (<code>sleep 60 ±20%</code>), the <i>distribution</i> of inter-arrival times stays tight and unimodal — nothing a human does looks like that over hours. Detectors score the coefficient of variation of intervals (beacon ≈ 0–0.35, human ≫ 1), the fraction within ±25% of the mean, and an autocorrelation/FFT peak at the period.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>RITA and Zeek score exactly this: connection-interval regularity + data-size consistency + connection count. Only genuinely aperiodic C2 defeats timing analysis — and that costs the operator responsiveness.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: jitter-resistant beacon detection', '../encrypted-anomaly/step3_beacon_detect.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: stacks combine <b>rules</b> (SNI/cert mismatch, JA3 rarity), <b>supervised ML</b> on flow features (traffic classification, known-family C2), <b>unsupervised baselining</b> ("this host never did this"), and <b>sequence models</b> on the first-N packet sizes. Honest limits: padding and traffic shaping (Tor, QUIC, MASQUE) blunt size/timing; <b>ECH</b> removes SNI and the JA3 inputs (fall back to flow features + JARM + reputation); and the <b>base-rate problem</b> means a 0.1% false-positive rate on 10M flows/day is 10,000 alerts — tune per-environment and pair with enrichment + triage automation.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Malleable C2 profiles, domain fronting, and "C2 over Slack/GitHub/Google Docs" all specifically target these detectors — the output should be a scored lead for an analyst, not an auto-block, until you have earned the precision.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Deep dive: TLS C2 hunts, exfil baselining, drift', '../encrypted-anomaly/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Hunting encrypted attacks with no decryption — full scenarios.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🕵️ Scenario — TLS C2 on a segment with no decryption</span><p>Threat intel: a loader using HTTPS C2 with a valid Let\'s Encrypt cert. You have pcap, no MITM. Run Zeek: (1) beaconing candidates — <code>(src,dst)</code> pairs with many small regular connections (<code>conn.log</code>, filter <code>orig_bytes&lt;1500 &amp;&amp; resp_bytes&lt;4000</code>, sort by count); (2) rare JA3 across the whole capture (<code>ssl.log</code>, <code>sort | uniq -c | sort -n | head</code>); (3) certs issued minutes ago, or CN≠SNI, or a dynamic-DNS SAN (<code>x509.log</code>). A <code>10.x→185.x</code> pair with 300 conns over 6 h at ~600 B up / 1.2 KB down, interval CV 0.18, a JA3 seen <b>nowhere else</b>, and a <code>*.workers.dev</code> cert behind an <code>api.company-cdn.com</code> SNI — any one is a lead; all three is a finding.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — exfil disguised as normal HTTPS</span><p>DLP (cleartext only) sees nothing; finance data hits a paste site a week later. A <b>per-host egress baseline</b> would have caught it: workstation <code>WS-4471</code> normally uploads &lt; 5 MB/day to O365 and the git server; one night it opens a single TLS flow to a personal cloud-storage domain and pushes 900 MB, <code>updown_ratio ≈ 250</code> — &gt; 30σ from its own history. Model each entity\'s own norm (population prior for cold start), and for "low and slow" also model the weekly aggregate and cumulative upload to <i>new</i> destinations.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Scenario — DoH hides both C2 and the domain.</b> Detect the DoH itself: to a non-sanctioned resolver it is a policy signal (known DoH IP/SNI list + the shape: many tiny request/response pairs to one host). Block it or force the enterprise resolver; disable app-level DoH via policy; alert on outbound :853. And watch <b>model drift</b>: an OS TLS-stack update shifts every JA3 — prefer JA4, retrain on a cadence with a labelled hold-out, feed analyst dispositions back as labels, and never page on a single ML score.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: 5 scenarios + the Zeek→features pipeline + operating notes', '../encrypted-anomaly/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'TLS 1.3 hides the payload. Name three signals a sensor still uses.', opts: ['Nothing — encrypted means invisible', 'Packet sizes/directions/timing (flow features), the ClientHello cipher/extension list (JA3/JA4), and connection-interval regularity (beaconing)', 'The decryption key from the certificate', 'Only the destination port'], ok: 1, why: 'Metadata and handshake shape survive encryption. Flow features classify behaviour, JA3/JA4 fingerprints the client stack, and interval regularity exposes C2 beacons — all without decryption.' },
    { q: 'What does a JA3/JA4 fingerprint identify?', opts: ['The user\'s identity', 'The client\'s TLS library/stack — its ordered cipher suites, extensions, curves and ALPN — which differs between browsers, curl, Go, and malware toolkits', 'The server\'s private key', 'The content of the first HTTP request'], ok: 1, why: 'The ClientHello is built by the TLS implementation, not the user. Hashing its structure gives a stable signature per stack, so an egress allowlist of sanctioned-software fingerprints flags unknown ones.' },
    { q: 'An implant beacons every 300s ±30% jitter. Still detectable from timing?', opts: ['No — jitter makes it random', 'Usually yes: the inter-arrival distribution stays bounded and unimodal around the mean period, with a low-ish CV and a high fraction within ±25% of the mean — unlike human traffic', 'Only if you decrypt it', 'Only with a signature for that malware family'], ok: 1, why: 'Jitter widens intervals but keeps them clustered around the period. Low coefficient of variation + tight histogram + connections at all hours + consistent tiny payloads still separate it from bursty, heavy-tailed human activity.' } ] },

/* ================= 15 · FRAUD & CIRCUMVENTION DETECTION ================= */
{ num: 15, emoji: '🎭', title: 'Fraud & Circumvention Detection', layer: 'Detection',
  tagline: 'Judging behaviour you cannot fully observe: velocity rules and impossible travel, linkage graphs that expose a ring, device/bot/proxy tells, and evaluating it in dollars.',
  apps: ['💳 card testing / chargebacks', '🎁 referral-bonus abuse', '👥 Sybil / multi-accounting'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>Fraud is <b>rare</b> (0.1–3%), the costs are <b>asymmetric</b> (a missed chargeback ≫ a false decline that also loses a good customer), and the adversary <b>adapts</b> — one person pretending to be a thousand, a bot shaped like a human, a stolen card tested in $1 increments.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A bouncer does not read minds. They watch: is this the tenth person tonight in the same distinctive jacket? Did someone just hand their wristband over the fence? Is that ID\'s birthday suspiciously round?</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"Accuracy" is a trap here: a model that says "never fraud" scores 98%+. You report precision/recall/FPR at a fixed review budget and pick the threshold by expected cost.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: velocity rules + impossible travel', '../fraud-detection/step1_velocity_rules.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Velocity rules</b> are the cheap 80%: signups per IP/hour, payments per card/day, distinct cards per account, distinct accounts per card/device, failed-auth bursts — plus <b>impossible travel</b> (two authenticated sessions farther apart than any flight could cover in the elapsed time).</p>' +
    '<p>Key insight: a <i>single</i> account in isolation looks fine. The same account inside a ring trips <b>shared-key</b> velocity — one device used by 12 signups in an hour, one card across 6 accounts — because those are <i>ring</i> features, not user features.</p>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: a ring lights up rules one user never does', '../fraud-detection/step1_velocity_rules.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Linkage graphs</b>: nodes are accounts, edges are shared <i>strong</i> identifiers (device fingerprint, payment/payout instrument, hashed phone/SSN, cookie, address, referral parent). Edge weight = how <i>surprising</i> the match is. Connected components above a size/strength threshold are multi-accounting, Sybil referral abuse, or a bust-out ring.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>One person buying one concert ticket is normal. One card buying 400 tickets in 90 seconds across 30 "different" accounts on the same phone is a scalper bot — and every shared-identifier edge says so.</p></div>' +
    '<p>Do <b>not</b> use shared IP as an edge — households, offices and CGNAT collapse the graph into one giant blob. IP is context, not a link.</p>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: linkage graph — rings vs households', '../fraud-detection/step2_device_graph.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Device fingerprinting</b> combines dozens of weak signals (canvas/WebGL/audio quirks, fonts, timezone, <code>navigator</code> fields, JA3/JA4) into one fairly-stable id — because cookies get cleared and IPs rotate. <b>Bot tells</b>: <code>navigator.webdriver</code>, no mouse entropy, superhuman form-fill speed, datacenter ASN. <b>Proxy/VPN evasion</b> shows up as timezone/locale/geo mismatch and brief residential-IP reuse across "unrelated" accounts.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>None of these is proof — privacy-conscious users exist. They are <i>risk features</i> that raise the score and can trigger a step-up challenge (3-D Secure, OTP, ID check), not an auto-block.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Deep dive: card testing, ATO, Sybil, false declines', '../fraud-detection/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: with a ~1% base rate, choose the threshold by <b>expected cost</b> = <code>FN·(chargeback+goods+fee+ops) + FP·(review + P(lost good customer)·CLV)</code>. The cost-optimal point is usually a <i>moderate</i> threshold with <i>partial</i> recall — chasing the last few percent of fraud costs more in friction and review than it saves. Then use <b>layered actions</b> (allow / challenge / review / block) and a <b>feedback loop</b>: chargebacks and analyst labels retrain the model. Watch for <b>feedback bias</b> — you only see outcomes for transactions you allowed — so keep a tiny random-allow holdout.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Most "fraud" in referral-bonus abuse is really an <b>incentive-design</b> bug: pay the bonus after a real purchase / 30-day retention, cap it per device and payout instrument, and the detector just buys you time.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: pick the threshold by dollars, not accuracy', '../fraud-detection/step3_eval_costs.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The fraud patterns you will actually be handed.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🎭 Scenario — referral-bonus abuse ($20/friend, 4,000 "friends")</span><p>Growth reports a great week; finance reports 12× budget; the "new users" never return. Group signups by device fingerprint, payout instrument, and referral parent: the linkage graph shows one component of 3,800 accounts joined by ~40 devices and 6 payout accounts, created in a 9-day window, each doing exactly the minimum qualifying action. <b>The real fix is the incentive</b>: pay the bonus after a real purchase / 30-day retention, cap per device and per payout instrument, add a step-up at claim time. The detector just buys time.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — account takeover, then a "trusted" fraudulent order</span><p>A long-tenured account ships a high-value order to a new address, paid with the card on file. Simple models miss it because the <i>account</i> is trusted and the <i>card</i> is real — the fraud is the <b>session</b>: login from a new device + new ASN + new geo, impossible travel vs the last good session, then a scripted burst (change email, add address, place order) with machine-fast timing. Score the session; step-up on address/payout changes independently of checkout; notify the <i>old</i> contact channel.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Scenario — Sybil farming behind residential proxies.</b> Each account gets a fresh ISP IP and an anti-detect browser profile, defeating IP reputation. What still links them: identical workflow timing (a behavioural fingerprint), payout convergence to a few cash-out accounts, near-duplicate content, brief IP reuse across "unrelated" accounts, and geo/latency inconsistency. And the failure mode nobody watches: <b>false declines</b> — a threshold tuned to minimise fraud loss alone forfeits good customers\' CLV; fold <code>P(lost good customer)·CLV</code> into the FP cost and move the mid-risk band to a challenge, not a block.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: 6 scenarios (bonus abuse, card testing, ATO, Sybil, drift, false declines) + metric reference', '../fraud-detection/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why is "accuracy" the wrong metric for a fraud model?', opts: ['It is too hard to compute', 'Fraud is ~0.1–3% of events, so a model that always predicts "not fraud" scores 97–99.9% while catching nothing', 'Accuracy ignores the training set', 'Regulators forbid it'], ok: 1, why: 'Under extreme class imbalance, accuracy is dominated by the easy negatives. Use precision, recall, FPR and PR-AUC at a fixed review budget, and ultimately optimise expected dollar cost.' },
    { q: 'A single account looks clean but is part of a bonus-abuse ring. What feature exposes it?', opts: ['Its account age', 'Shared-key velocity/linkage — the device, card, or payout instrument it shares is used by many "unrelated" accounts', 'Its password strength', 'The browser language'], ok: 1, why: 'Ring signal lives between accounts, not in one. Aggregating over the shared identifier (device/card/payout) reveals the fan-out; a linkage graph turns it into one high-risk component.' },
    { q: 'Why not use "shared IP address" as an edge in a linkage graph?', opts: ['IPs change too slowly', 'Households, offices, CGNAT and mobile carriers put thousands of unrelated users behind one IP — using it as an edge merges the whole graph into one component', 'IP addresses are encrypted', 'It is illegal to log IPs'], ok: 1, why: 'Strong identifiers (device fingerprint, payment instrument, hashed SSN, cookie) make good edges. Shared IP is a weak context feature; as a hard link it destroys the graph\'s signal.' } ] },

/* ================= 16 · KEY-MANAGEMENT INFRASTRUCTURE ================= */
{ num: 16, emoji: '🗝️', title: 'Key-Management Infrastructure', layer: 'Security',
  tagline: 'The vault under everything: KEK/DEK hierarchies, KMS/HSM, envelope encryption, crypto-shredding, Shamir secret sharing, and rotating keys without an outage.',
  apps: ['🔐 encryption at rest everywhere', '🧨 GDPR "delete my data"', '🎫 JWT / mTLS / CA signing'],
  levels: [
  { html: '<p>Encryption is easy. <b>Key management is the hard 90%</b> — a key generated badly, stored next to the data, never rotated, or impossible to revoke gives you the cost of cryptography with none of the protection.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>A bank does not hide its gold in a bigger drawer. It builds a vault: one hardened room, a locked hierarchy of doors, a rule that no one person can open it, a log of every visit, and a plan for changing the locks without closing the bank.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>AWS KMS, GCP Cloud KMS, Azure Key Vault and Vault Transit are all the same pattern: you call Encrypt/Decrypt/Sign, and the key never leaves the HSM.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: envelope encryption, KEK rotation, crypto-shred', '../key-management/step1_envelope_encryption.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Envelope encryption</b>: a per-object <b>DEK</b> (data key) encrypts the data; a <b>KEK</b> (key-encryption key) in the KMS/HSM wraps the DEK; the wrapped DEK is stored next to the ciphertext. To read: one small KMS call to unwrap the DEK, then decrypt locally.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>HSM master key  (never leaves the device)\n  wraps -> KEK   (per app / tenant / env)\n    wraps -> DEK  (per object / table / file)\n      encrypts -> data</code></pre>' +
    '<p>This makes <b>KEK rotation cheap</b> — re-wrap the DEKs, do not re-encrypt petabytes (cost is O(number of keys)) — and shrinks the blast radius: a leaked DEK exposes <i>one</i> object.</p>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: rotation re-wraps keys, not terabytes', '../key-management/step1_envelope_encryption.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p><b>Crypto-shredding</b>: destroy a key and its data is instantly, provably unrecoverable — even across immutable backups and replicas, where the ciphertext still sits. So "delete this user\'s data" (GDPR erasure across 40 backup snapshots) can mean "destroy this user\'s key" — <i>if</i> the data was encrypted under a per-user key from the start.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p>You cannot un-print a million copies of a document, but if every copy is in a language only one lost dictionary could translate, burning the dictionary is as good as shredding them all.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>Key destruction itself must be irreversible and logged — no 30-day undelete for that key — and every replicated key store must purge it.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: destroy the key, data is shredded', '../key-management/step1_envelope_encryption.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>The root/unseal key must not exist whole anywhere. <b>Shamir\'s Secret Sharing</b> splits it into <code>n</code> shares such that any <code>k</code> reconstruct it and any <code>k-1</code> reveal <b>nothing</b>. This is how Vault unseals and how offline CA roots are protected: <code>n=5</code> custodians, quorum <code>k=3</code>, so two shares can be lost (availability) or stolen (confidentiality) without failure.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>secret = f(0) of a random degree-(k-1) polynomial\nshare i = (i, f(i)) ;  any k points -> f  (Lagrange)\n                        k-1 points  -> f(0) uniformly random</code></pre>' +
    '<p>Plain Shamir does not detect a forged share — a wrong share yields a wrong key <i>silently</i>. Real systems add a per-share commitment (VSS) or check a Key Check Value.</p>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: k-of-n secret sharing, the key ceremony', '../key-management/step2_shamir_split.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Expert view: rotating a <b>signing</b> key (JWT, mTLS CA, package/DNSSEC) needs an <b>overlap window</b> where both keys verify — publish the new key, wait for propagation (JWKS TTL), switch signing, then wait <code>max_token_life + skew</code> before retiring the old one. Retire too early and valid in-flight tokens are rejected (an outage that looks like an auth bug); keep token lifetime <b>≪ crypto-period</b> or you can never retire on time. Automate it (KMS auto-rotate, Vault, cert-manager) so it is routine, not a feared annual event.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📱 In your daily life</span><p>"Secret zero" — the app needs a credential to fetch its credentials — is solved with platform identity the workload did not have to be handed: a Kubernetes projected ServiceAccount token, a cloud IAM role, a SPIFFE SVID — the secrets manager trusts that and issues short-lived leased secrets.</p></div>',
    try: [['🔬 Lab: signing-key rotation windows & crypto-periods', '../key-management/step3_rotation_windows.py', 'o']] },
  { html: '<p>Key management where the theory meets an outage.</p>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🗝️ Scenario — encrypt a 400 TB data lake, then rotate the KEK</span><p>One KEK per dataset in an HSM-backed KMS; a fresh DEK per object (or per 128 MB block), wrapped DEK in the object footer. Annual rotation: <code>KMS.rotate(KEK)</code> → a background job re-wraps existing DEKs (decrypt-old, encrypt-new — kilobytes each), <b>not the 400 TB</b>. Gotchas: KMS request quotas (spread the re-wrap, do not run it all Monday), backups written under v1 need the old KEK version kept <i>enabled for decrypt</i>, and cross-region readers add a latency + failure domain — use multi-region keys.</p></div>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">🚨 Scenario — JWT key rotation breaks logins for 20 minutes</span><p>Ops swapped <code>kid=v1</code> for <code>v2</code> and immediately removed <code>v1</code> from the JWKS. Tokens already signed with <code>v1</code> were valid for up to 59 more minutes; every verifier that refreshed its cache rejected them. Correct order: publish <code>v2</code> alongside <code>v1</code> (keep signing <code>v1</code>) → wait ≥ JWKS TTL + fetch → switch signing to <code>v2</code> → wait ≥ max token lifetime + skew → remove <code>v1</code>. Dual-<b>verify</b> window ≥ <code>JWKS_TTL + max_token_life + skew</code>; keep token life ≪ crypto-period or step 4 never fits.</p></div>' +
    '<p><b>Scenario — GDPR erasure across 40 immutable backups.</b> You cannot rewrite the snapshots — so encrypt each user\'s data under a <b>per-user key</b> from the start and make erasure "destroy that key" (irreversibly, logged, on every replica). The ciphertext stays everywhere, permanently unreadable. And <b>secret zero</b> — the app needs a credential to fetch its credentials — is solved with platform identity it did not have to be handed: a projected Kubernetes ServiceAccount token, a cloud IAM role, a SPIFFE SVID; the secrets manager trusts that and issues short-lived leased secrets.</p>',
    try: [['📖 Full deep dive: 6 scenarios (data-lake rotation, JWT outage, HSM failover, secret zero, crypto-shred, CA ceremony)', '../key-management/DEEP_DIVE.md', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'In envelope encryption, why rotate the KEK instead of re-encrypting all the data?', opts: ['Re-encrypting is illegal', 'Rotation only has to re-wrap the DEKs (kilobytes each) under the new KEK — the object ciphertext is untouched, so cost is O(number of keys), not O(bytes)', 'The KEK never actually changes', 'DEKs cannot be rotated'], ok: 1, why: 'Each object keeps its DEK; only the small wrapped-DEK blobs are decrypted-with-old and encrypted-with-new. A petabyte store rotates in the time it takes to re-wrap its key manifests.' },
    { q: 'How does crypto-shredding satisfy "delete my data" across 40 immutable backups?', opts: ['It rewrites every backup', 'The data was encrypted under a per-user key; destroying that key leaves the ciphertext in every backup permanently unreadable', 'Backups are exempt from erasure requests', 'It emails the backup vendor'], ok: 1, why: 'You cannot rewrite immutable snapshots, but you do not need to — without the (irreversibly destroyed, per-user) key, the ciphertext everywhere is just noise. It only works if the key was per-user from the start.' },
    { q: 'Rotating a JWT signing key, ops removed the old key from the JWKS immediately after switching. What breaks?', opts: ['Nothing', 'Tokens already signed with the old key are still valid and in flight (up to max token lifetime) — verifiers that refreshed the JWKS now reject them, causing 401s until they expire', 'The new key stops working', 'The database goes read-only'], ok: 1, why: 'You need a dual-VERIFY overlap of at least JWKS_TTL + max_token_life + clock_skew. Publish new, wait, switch signing, wait, then retire old. And keep token lifetime well below the crypto-period.' } ] }

];
